ForgeData.register("cs",{"CS-1":{"label":"Fundamentals of Programming","color":"#0f766e","questions":[{"id":"CS-01","stem":"A student writes a loop that should run 10 times but runs 11. This is most likely caused by:","options":{"A":"An off-by-one error","B":"A syntax error — the code will not run at all.","C":"The computer's RAM is insufficient.","D":"The programming language doesn't support loops."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Off-by-one errors: a FOR loop from 0 to 10 inclusive runs 11 times (0,1,2...10). A loop from 0 to <10 runs 10 times. Classic trap: 'for i in range(10)' runs 0-9 (10 iterations); 'for i in range(1,11)' runs 1-10 (10 iterations). Always check: does the boundary condition include or exclude the final value? Trace through the first and last iteration manually.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the output of: for i in range(1, 6): print(i) ?","options":{"A":"Compilers are faster to write programs with; interpreters produce faster programs.","B":"0 1 2 3 4 5","C":"1 2 3 4 5 (range(1,6) generates 1,2,3,4,5 — the stop value 6 is excluded)","D":"1 2 3 4"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-02","stem":"Which correctly distinguishes a compiler from an interpreter?","options":{"A":"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm.","B":"A compiler translates the whole program first; an interpreter translates line by line.","C":"Compilers only work with low-level languages; interpreters only with high-level.","D":"An interpreter creates object code; a compiler creates source code."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Compiler: whole program → machine code → executable. Advantages: fast execution, code protected (source not visible), errors caught before running. Disadvantages: slow compilation, platform-specific. Interpreter: line by line → execute immediately. Advantages: easier to debug (stops at error), cross-platform, no compilation step. Disadvantages: slower runtime, source code visible. Python = interpreted. C/C++ = compiled. Java = compiled to bytecode then interpreted by JVM.","reforge":{"stem":"Python programs are slower to execute than equivalent C programs. The primary reason is:","options":{"A":"Python uses more RAM than C.","B":"C programs are shorter than Python programs.","C":"Python was designed for beginners and deliberately made slow.","D":"Python is interpreted at runtime; C is compiled"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-03","stem":"In a binary search, what is the maximum number of comparisons needed to find an element in a sorted list of 1024 items?","options":{"A":"Up to 1024 comparisons — binary search checks every element.","B":"Up to 512 comparisons — it halves the list but still needs half the elements.","C":"Up to 10 comparisons — binary search is O(log₂n) and log₂1024 = 10.","D":"Up to 32 comparisons."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Binary search: compare target with middle element. If equal, found. If less, search lower half. If greater, search upper half. Each comparison halves the search space. After k comparisons, at most 2^k items remain to search. For n items: max comparisons = log₂n. 1024 = 2¹⁰, so max = 10. Linear search: max = n (1024). Binary search requires sorted data. Merge sort has O(n log n) — learn these complexity classes.","reforge":{"stem":"A linear search of 1000 items requires at most 1000 comparisons. A binary search requires at most 10. Which algorithm is more efficient for large datasets and why?","options":{"A":"Binary search — O(log n) vs O(n) complexity means it scales dramatically better for large datasets, though it requires sorted data.","B":"Linear search — it works on unsorted data.","C":"Both are equally efficient for large datasets, which ignores that binary search discards half the remaining items at every single comparison step while linear search does not.","D":"Linear search — it always finds the item faster."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-04","stem":"What does it mean for an algorithm to have O(n²) time complexity?","options":{"A":"Up to 512 comparisons — it halves the list but still needs half the elements, a description of binary search's behaviour rather than what O(n squared) time complexity means.","B":"The algorithm runs in exactly n² milliseconds.","C":"The algorithm uses n² bytes of memory.","D":"The algorithm's running time grows proportionally to the square of the input size n"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Big O notation describes how runtime scales with input size, not exact time. O(1) = constant. O(log n) = logarithmic (binary search). O(n) = linear (linear search). O(n log n) = merge sort, quicksort average. O(n²) = bubble sort, selection sort, insertion sort worst case — nested loops. O(2^n) = exponential (brute force combinatorial problems). Eduqas expects you to identify complexity from algorithms and compare efficiency.","reforge":{"stem":"Bubble sort compares adjacent elements and swaps them repeatedly. For n items, it may perform up to n² comparisons. What is its time complexity?","options":{"A":"Subroutines can only be used once; programs can be reused, a claim about code reuse unrelated to bubble sort's time complexity.","B":"O(n²) — the nested loop structure (compare each element with each other) means comparisons scale as the square of n.","C":"O(log n)","D":"O(n log n)"},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-05","stem":"A student says: 'A stack and a queue are the same — both store data.' What is the key difference?","options":{"A":"A stack is LIFO (Last In First Out)","B":"There is no difference.","C":"A queue can store more items than a stack.","D":"Stacks are used in hardware; queues are software only."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Stack (LIFO): push adds to top, pop removes from top. Used for: function call stack (return addresses), undo operations, expression evaluation. Queue (FIFO): enqueue adds to back, dequeue removes from front. Used for: print queues, BFS graph traversal, CPU scheduling, buffering. A priority queue removes the highest priority item regardless of order — different from both.","reforge":{"stem":"A web browser's 'back' button returns to the previously visited page. Which data structure best models this behaviour and why?","options":{"A":"A queue — because pages are visited in order.","B":"A linked list — because pages can be revisited in any order.","C":"A stack, since back pops the most recent page","D":"An array — because pages are accessed by index number."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-06","stem":"Which correctly describes the difference between a subroutine (function) and a program?","options":{"A":"There is no difference — both do the same thing.","B":"A subroutine is a reusable, named block of code for one task within a program.","C":"Subroutines can only be used once; programs can be reused.","D":"Compilers only work with low-level languages; interpreters only with high-level."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Subroutines (functions/procedures): named blocks, called with parameters, may return values. Benefits: reduce code duplication, easier to test and debug individual components, improve readability, enable team development. Procedures: don't return values. Functions: return values. Passing parameters: by value (copy — original unchanged), by reference (original changed). Eduqas expects you to write and trace subroutines.","reforge":{"stem":"A function is passed a variable 'by reference' rather than 'by value.' What is the key difference?","options":{"A":"By reference is slower than by value.","B":"There is no practical difference.","C":"By value is only used for integers; by reference for strings. The computing explanation must match the code, data, protocol, algorithm or hardware mechanism described; the answer does not account for the competing factor or trade-off described.","D":"By reference passes the memory address — changes inside the function affect the original variable. By value passes a copy — the original is unaffected. By reference is more memory efficient but risks unintended side effects."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-07","stem":"In the context of networking, what is the role of the TCP/IP protocol suite?","options":{"A":"Is faster because it sends data without waiting for acknowledgement.","B":"It encrypts all data sent over the internet.","C":"Provides protocols for addressing (IP) and transmission (TCP).","D":"It provides wireless connectivity to devices."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"TCP/IP model layers: Application (HTTP, SMTP, FTP), Transport (TCP — reliable/ordered; UDP — fast/unreliable), Internet (IP — addressing and routing), Network Access (physical transmission). IP addresses identify devices; MAC addresses identify hardware. Routers operate at the IP layer. TCP handshake establishes connection. UDP used for streaming/gaming where speed > reliability.","reforge":{"stem":"A video streaming service uses UDP rather than TCP. The main reason is:","options":{"A":"UDP prioritises speed over reliability — a few dropped packets cause minor video glitches rather than delays. TCP's retransmission of lost packets would cause frustrating pauses, which are worse than minor quality drops.","B":"UDP is more secure than TCP.","C":"TCP cannot handle video data.","D":"Python is interpreted line by line at runtime whereas C is compiled directly to machine code ahead of execution — this interpretation overhead is a completely separate issue from why streaming services choose UDP over TCP."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-08","stem":"A student writes: 'Encryption makes data impossible to read.' Why is this imprecise?","options":{"A":"Encryption is only used by governments.","B":"It is correct — encrypted data cannot be read.","C":"Both sender and receiver use the same secret key, shared securely in advance.","D":"Encryption transforms data so it cannot be READ WITHOUT the correct key"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Encryption types: symmetric (same key for encryption and decryption — AES, fast, key distribution problem), asymmetric (public key encrypts, private key decrypts — RSA, HTTPS, slower). HTTPS uses asymmetric to exchange symmetric key, then symmetric for speed. Hashing is not encryption (one-way, cannot decrypt — used for passwords). Steganography is hiding data, not encrypting it.","reforge":{"stem":"HTTPS uses both asymmetric and symmetric encryption. Why are both needed rather than just one?","options":{"A":"Asymmetric encryption is illegal for commercial use.","B":"Asymmetric encryption (RSA) is used to securely exchange a symmetric key (AES) — then the faster symmetric encryption handles actual data. Using asymmetric for all data would be too slow.","C":"Symmetric encryption cannot handle web traffic.","D":"One-way and difficult to reverse, so recovering the original input is computationally impractical — a description of hashing, not an explanation of why HTTPS actually combines two different encryption types together."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C1A-01","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Decomposition in computational thinking means:","options":{"A":"breaking a complex problem into manageable sub-problems","B":"Converting decimal to binary, a separate number-representation skill unrelated to what decomposition means in computational thinking.","C":"writing code without testing","D":"using only one algorithm"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"Decomposition reduces complexity and supports separate development and testing.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: Decomposition in computational thinking means:","options":{"A":"breaking a complex problem into manageable sub-problems","B":"The loop is equivalent to a FOR loop in every possible case.","C":"The condition is evaluated only after the first full iteration.","D":"Global scope, so it is destroyed after declaration."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-C1A-02","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Abstraction involves:","options":{"A":"The loop is equivalent to a FOR loop in every possible case.","B":"removing unnecessary detail while retaining what matters","C":"copying a program exactly","D":"converting decimal to binary"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"An abstraction focuses attention on relevant features.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: Abstraction involves:","options":{"A":"log₂ n, because the list is repeatedly halved.","B":"removing unnecessary detail while retaining what matters","C":"The condition is evaluated only after the first full iteration.","D":"1, because the item is found immediately."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-C1A-03","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A syntax error is usually detected:","options":{"A":"only after a user accepts the output","B":"by a network router","C":"when the code is parsed or compiled","D":"by a database administrator"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Syntax errors violate language rules and prevent valid translation or execution.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A syntax error is usually detected:","options":{"A":"An interpreter only works with assembly language source code.","B":"Always sorted into ascending order automatically.","C":"when the code is parsed or compiled","D":"Stored on disk rather than in main memory."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1A-04","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A semantic error occurs when:","options":{"A":"a variable has no name","B":"the keyboard is disconnected","C":"An interpreter creates object code; a compiler creates source code.","D":"the program runs but does not do what was intended"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Semantics concerns meaning and behaviour rather than grammar alone.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: A semantic error occurs when:","options":{"A":"Post-order traversal.","B":"Breadth-first traversal.","C":"Python was designed for beginners and deliberately made slow.","D":"the program runs but does not do what was intended"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-05","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A test case should include:","options":{"A":"input data, expected output and the actual result","B":"only the programmer's name","C":"Subroutines can only be used once; programs can be reused.","D":"the source code of every rival"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Test records make failures reproducible and support evaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A test case should include:","options":{"A":"input data, expected output and the actual result","B":"Deletes the method from the superclass permanently.","C":"Calls the superclass method twice in succession.","D":"Prevents any further subclasses being created."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-06","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Boundary testing checks values:","options":{"A":"chosen only from the middle of the range","B":"at and around the limits of valid input","C":"that can never be entered","D":"used only for graphics"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Many errors occur at lower, upper and just-outside boundaries.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Boundary testing checks values:","options":{"A":"Removes the need for any binary conversion.","B":"at and around the limits of valid input","C":"Reduce the file size while improving the sound quality.","D":"Increase the number of bits used for each individual sample."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C1A-07","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A pre-condition states:","options":{"A":"A syntax error — the code will not run at all.","B":"the processor clock speed","C":"what must be true before an operation begins","D":"the colour of an interface"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Pre-conditions define assumptions required for safe execution.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A pre-condition states:","options":{"A":"Addition is not defined for any numeric values.","B":"Quotation marks always denote a comment line.","C":"what must be true before an operation begins","D":"Data type stored at that position."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-08","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A post-condition states:","options":{"A":"Programs are written in high-level languages; subroutines in low-level.","B":"the name of the compiler","C":"the number of network hops","D":"what should be true after an operation completes"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Post-conditions help specify and test the result of an operation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A post-condition states:","options":{"A":"Compilers are faster to write programs with; interpreters produce faster programs.","B":"Compilers only work with low-level languages; interpreters only with high-level.","C":"An interpreter creates object code; a compiler creates source code.","D":"what should be true after an operation completes"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-09","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A recursive algorithm must include:","options":{"A":"a base case that stops further calls","B":"Up to 1024 comparisons — binary search checks every element.","C":"a network connection","D":"a database table"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Without a base case, recursive calls may continue until resources are exhausted.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A recursive algorithm must include:","options":{"A":"a base case that stops further calls","B":"Stacks are used in hardware; queues are software only.","C":"There is no difference — both do the same thing.","D":"Subroutines can only be used once; programs can be reused."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-C1A-10","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Binary search requires the data to be:","options":{"A":"encrypted","B":"sorted","C":"stored in a stack","D":"randomly ordered"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Each comparison discards half the remaining sorted search space.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: Binary search requires the data to be:","options":{"A":"writing code without testing","B":"sorted","C":"using only one algorithm","D":"adding every possible detail to a model"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-11","spec":"CS-1","stem":"The worst-case complexity of linear search is:","options":{"A":"O(1)","B":"O(log n) always","C":"O(n)","D":"O(n²) always"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"A linear search may inspect every item once.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: The worst-case complexity of linear search is:","options":{"A":"only the programmer's name","B":"a guarantee that the system is correct","C":"O(n)","D":"the source code of every rival"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-12","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A queue uses which access rule?","options":{"A":"last in, first out","B":"random in, random out","C":"highest priority only","D":"first in, first out"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"The earliest item added is removed first.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A queue uses which access rule?","options":{"A":"the number of network hops","B":"a global variable in every function","C":"a network connection","D":"first in, first out"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-13","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A linked list stores each node with:","options":{"A":"data and a link or pointer to another node","B":"only a fixed numeric index","C":"Stacks are used in hardware; queues are software only.","D":"a public encryption key only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Links allow nodes to be connected without contiguous memory.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A linked list stores each node with:","options":{"A":"data and a link or pointer to another node","B":"A queue — because pages are visited in order.","C":"highest priority only","D":"a flat sequence only"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-14","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A tree with no cycles is useful for representing:","options":{"A":"a flat sequence only","B":"hierarchical relationships","C":"A queue can store more items than a stack.","D":"an analogue voltage"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"File systems and organisation charts are common hierarchical examples.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A tree with no cycles is useful for representing:","options":{"A":"one variable to hold no values","B":"hierarchical relationships","C":"all classes to inherit from two parents","D":"a program to run without data"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-15","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A hash table is designed for:","options":{"A":"sorting every item by size","B":"storing only images","C":"fast average lookup using a key","D":"A linked list — because pages can be revisited in any order."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"A hash function maps keys to locations, although collisions need handling.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A hash table is designed for:","options":{"A":"measure processor temperature","B":"runs one source line and forgets it","C":"fast average lookup using a key","D":"stores files in a queue"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C1A-16","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Polymorphism allows:","options":{"A":"one variable to hold no values","B":"Up to 512 comparisons — it halves the list but still needs half the elements.","C":"a program to run without data","D":"one interface or method name to work with objects of different types"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-OOP","scaffold":"Polymorphism supports flexible object-oriented designs.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: Polymorphism allows:","options":{"A":"Static typing, which prevents external access.","B":"Been passed by reference to the calling routine.","C":"An interpreter produces a standalone executable; a compiler does not.","D":"one interface or method name to work with objects of different types"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1A-17","spec":"CS-1","stem":"Encapsulation means:","options":{"A":"bundling data with methods and controlling access to the data","B":"Allowing one method name to behave differently in different classes.","C":"removing all classes","D":"using only public variables"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-OOP","scaffold":"Encapsulation protects an object's state through a defined interface.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: Encapsulation means:","options":{"A":"bundling data with methods and controlling access to the data","B":"A stack is LIFO (Last In First Out), a data-structure fact unrelated to what encapsulation means in object-oriented programming.","C":"Too large for the algorithm to process.","D":"The attribute occupies less memory when the object is created."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-18","spec":"CS-1","stem":"An invariant is:","options":{"A":"By value is only used for integers; by reference for strings.","B":"a condition that remains true during an algorithm or loop","C":"a syntax error in a class","D":"a type of network cable"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Loop invariants help reason about correctness.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: An invariant is:","options":{"A":"Limited to a maximum of 256 elements.","B":"a condition that remains true during an algorithm or loop","C":"Assigned the highest priority value.","D":"An interpreter only works with assembly language source code."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-19","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A dry run is used to:","options":{"A":"The programming language doesn't support loops.","B":"encrypt a database","C":"trace an algorithm manually with chosen values","D":"measure processor temperature"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Tracing variables step by step can reveal logic errors.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A dry run is used to:","options":{"A":"Its position expressed as an array index.","B":"A checksum used to verify the data.","C":"trace an algorithm manually with chosen values","D":"Creating a new class that inherits from an existing one."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-C1A-20","spec":"CS-1","stem":"A compiler generally:","options":{"A":"Deletes the method from the superclass permanently.","B":"stores files in a queue","C":"routes packets across a network","D":"translates a whole source program before execution"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Compilation can produce object or executable code and report errors before running.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A compiler generally:","options":{"A":"Reduce the file size while improving the sound quality.","B":"01011010","C":"00110101","D":"translates a whole source program before execution"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C1A-21","spec":"CS-1","stem":"An interpreter generally:","options":{"A":"translates and executes source code incrementally","B":"Increase the number of bits used for each individual sample.","C":"only checks spelling","D":"controls a hard drive motor"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Interpretation supports interactive testing but may add runtime overhead.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: An interpreter generally:","options":{"A":"translates and executes source code incrementally","B":"Have no effect unless the bit depth is also reduced.","C":"Always produces a smaller file than lossy compression does.","D":"Can only be applied to text files and never to images."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-01","stem":"A programmer uses a WHILE loop where the condition is checked before the body executes. Which statement is correct?","options":{"A":"The body always executes at least once regardless of the condition.","B":"The body may never execute if the condition is false initially","C":"The loop is equivalent to a FOR loop in every possible case.","D":"The condition is evaluated only after the first full iteration."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"WHILE is a pre-conditioned loop: the condition is tested first, so if it is false at the start the body runs zero times. REPEAT...UNTIL (or do-while) is post-conditioned: the body runs once before any test, so it always executes at least once. FOR is a count-controlled loop used when the number of iterations is known in advance. Choosing the wrong one is a common source of logic errors that produce no error message.","reforge":{"stem":"Which loop construct guarantees its body runs at least once?","options":{"A":"A WHILE loop, because the condition is checked first.","B":"A nested loop, because the inner body is always reached.","C":"A FOR loop, because the count is fixed before entry.","D":"A REPEAT...UNTIL loop, tested at the end"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-02","stem":"A variable declared inside a subroutine cannot be accessed from the main program. This is because it has:","options":{"A":"Global scope, so it is destroyed after declaration.","B":"Static typing, which prevents external access.","C":"Local scope, so it exists only in that subroutine","D":"Been passed by reference to the calling routine."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"Scope determines where an identifier is visible. Local variables exist only inside the subroutine that declares them and are destroyed when it returns, so the same name can be reused elsewhere without collision. Global variables are visible throughout the program, which makes them convenient but risky: any routine can modify them, making bugs hard to trace. Good practice is to keep variables local and communicate through parameters and return values.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is heavy use of global variables generally discouraged?","options":{"A":"Any part of the program can change them, hiding bugs","B":"They use significantly more memory than local variables.","C":"They cannot be used inside subroutines at all.","D":"They are automatically destroyed between statements."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-03","stem":"Passing a parameter by reference rather than by value means the subroutine receives:","options":{"A":"A copy of the data, so the original cannot be changed.","B":"Only the data type, with the value supplied later.","C":"The value converted into a string representation.","D":"The memory address, so changes affect the original"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"By value: a copy is made, so modifications inside the subroutine do not affect the caller's variable — safer, but uses more memory for large structures. By reference: the address is passed, so the subroutine works on the original — efficient for large arrays and allows multiple values to be returned, but changes can surprise the caller. Knowing which a language uses explains why some subroutines appear to modify their arguments and others do not.","reforge":{"stem":"A subroutine sorts a large array in place without returning it, and the caller sees the sorted result. The array was passed:","options":{"A":"By value, so a copy was sorted and discarded.","B":"By reference, so the original was modified","C":"As a constant, which cannot be modified.","D":"As a literal, which has no memory address."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N1-04","stem":"Which of these is a logic error rather than a syntax error?","options":{"A":"A program that runs but divides by the wrong count","B":"A missing closing bracket at the end of a statement.","C":"A misspelled keyword that the compiler cannot recognise.","D":"A missing semicolon in a language that requires one."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Syntax error: the code breaks the language's grammar, so it will not compile or interpret — the error is reported before execution. Logic error: the code is valid and runs, but produces the wrong result, so nothing is reported and only testing reveals it. Runtime error: the code is valid but fails during execution, such as division by zero or accessing an array index out of bounds. Logic errors are the hardest because the program appears to work.","reforge":{"stem":"A program crashes partway through with 'index out of range'. This is best classified as:","options":{"A":"A syntax error, because the code structure is invalid.","B":"A logic error, because the output is incorrect.","C":"A runtime error, because it occurs during execution","D":"A design error that prevents compilation."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-05","stem":"In a linear search of an unsorted list of n items, the worst-case number of comparisons is:","options":{"A":"log₂ n, because the list is repeatedly halved.","B":"n, because every item may need to be checked","C":"n², because each item is compared with every other.","D":"1, because the item is found immediately."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Linear search checks each element in turn: best case 1 comparison, worst case n, average n/2. It works on unsorted data. Binary search repeatedly halves a sorted list, giving a worst case of log₂ n — far better for large n, but it requires the data to be sorted first. So for a single search of unsorted data, linear search may be quicker overall than sorting and then binary searching.","reforge":{"stem":"Binary search on a sorted list of 1000 items requires at most about how many comparisons?","options":{"A":"1000, since each item may be checked in turn.","B":"500, since on average half the list is examined.","C":"3, since the list is divided into three parts.","D":"10, since log₂ 1000 is just under 10"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-06","stem":"A bubble sort makes a complete pass through a list without performing any swaps. This indicates that the list is:","options":{"A":"Reversed, so the sort must start again.","B":"Too large for the algorithm to process.","C":"Sorted, so the algorithm can stop early","D":"Corrupted, since a swap should always occur."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent pairs and swaps those out of order, so after each pass the largest remaining item settles at the end. A pass with no swaps means every adjacent pair is already in order, so the whole list is sorted — an optimised implementation uses a swapped flag to terminate then, giving a best case of O(n). The worst and average cases remain O(n²), which is why it is a teaching algorithm rather than a practical one.","reforge":{"stem":"Merge sort is generally preferred to bubble sort for large data sets because its time complexity is:","options":{"A":"O(n log n), which scales far better than O(n²)","B":"O(n²), which grows more slowly than bubble sort's O(n).","C":"O(1), because the time taken does not depend on n.","D":"O(log n), because the data is never fully traversed."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-07","stem":"A recursive subroutine must include a base case in order to:","options":{"A":"Improve the readability of the code for other programmers.","B":"Convert the recursion into an equivalent iteration.","C":"Allow the subroutine to accept more than one parameter.","D":"Stop the recursion, preventing a stack overflow"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Recursion needs two parts: a base case that returns without calling itself, and a recursive case that moves closer to the base case. Each call adds a stack frame holding parameters and the return address; without a reachable base case the stack grows until it overflows. Recursion often expresses tree and divide-and-conquer problems elegantly, but iteration is usually more memory-efficient since it needs no stack frames.","reforge":{"stem":"Compared with an equivalent iterative version, a recursive solution typically uses more:","options":{"A":"Disk storage, because each call is written to a file.","B":"Memory, because each call adds a stack frame","C":"Network bandwidth, because calls are sent remotely.","D":"Processor cores, because calls execute in parallel."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N1-08","stem":"What is the most accurate description of the difference between a compiler and an interpreter?","options":{"A":"A compiler translates all the code first; an interpreter line by line","B":"A compiler executes code directly without producing any output file.","C":"An interpreter produces a standalone executable; a compiler does not.","D":"An interpreter only works with assembly language source code."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Compiler: translates the entire source into object code in one go, reporting all errors together; the resulting executable runs quickly and without the source, but compilation takes time and the output is platform-specific. Interpreter: translates and executes one statement at a time, so errors surface at the point of failure and development is quicker, but execution is slower and the interpreter must be present. Assemblers translate assembly language into machine code one-to-one.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is an interpreter often preferred during program development?","options":{"A":"It produces faster-running code than a compiler does.","B":"It removes the need to test the program at all.","C":"Errors are reported as each statement runs, easing debugging","D":"It converts the program into platform-independent machine code."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-09","stem":"An array differs from a record (struct) in that an array normally holds items that are:","options":{"A":"Always sorted into ascending order automatically.","B":"All of the same data type, accessed by index","C":"Stored on disk rather than in main memory.","D":"Limited to a maximum of 256 elements."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Array: a fixed-size, ordered collection of elements of one type, accessed by numeric index, stored contiguously so element addresses can be calculated directly. Record: a collection of fields that may be of different types, accessed by field name — suited to modelling one entity such as a student. A list may be dynamic and hold mixed types depending on the language. Choosing correctly is about whether items are the same kind of thing or different attributes of one thing.","reforge":{"stem":"A programmer needs to store a student's name, date of birth and exam mark together. The most appropriate structure is:","options":{"A":"A one-dimensional array of integers.","B":"A queue, because the records arrive sequentially.","C":"A stack, because the data is added in order.","D":"A record, because the fields differ in type"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-10","stem":"A stack is described as a LIFO structure. This means the item removed is always the one that was:","options":{"A":"Assigned the highest priority value.","B":"Added first, at the base of the stack.","C":"Added most recently, at the top","D":"Stored at the lowest memory address."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Stack: Last In First Out, with push and pop operating at one end (the top) tracked by a stack pointer. Used for call return addresses, undo functionality and expression evaluation. Queue: First In First Out, with enqueue at the rear and dequeue at the front, tracked by two pointers. Used for print spooling and buffering. A circular queue reuses freed space at the front by wrapping the rear pointer round.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is best modelled by a queue rather than a stack?","options":{"A":"Managing print jobs in order of arrival","B":"Storing return addresses during subroutine calls.","C":"Implementing the undo function in an editor.","D":"Reversing the characters in a string."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-11","stem":"In a circular queue, the rear pointer wraps around to the start of the array in order to:","options":{"A":"Sort the items into the order they will be removed.","B":"Guarantee the queue can never become full.","C":"Convert the queue into a stack when required.","D":"Reuse space freed at the front by earlier removals"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"In a linear queue, repeated dequeues leave unusable empty space at the front while the rear pointer advances toward the end of the array. A circular queue treats the array as a ring: when the rear reaches the last index it wraps to index 0, reusing that space. Full and empty conditions must then be distinguished carefully, usually with a counter or by leaving one slot unused, since front and rear coincide in both states.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must a circular queue distinguish the full and empty states carefully?","options":{"A":"The array indices become negative when wrapping occurs.","B":"The front and rear pointers can be equal in both cases","C":"Items are stored in two arrays simultaneously.","D":"The queue reverses its order once it wraps around."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-12","stem":"Which traversal of a binary tree visits the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree?","options":{"A":"In-order traversal.","B":"Pre-order traversal.","C":"Post-order traversal.","D":"Breadth-first traversal."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Pre-order: node, left, right — used to copy a tree or produce prefix notation. In-order: left, node, right — on a binary search tree this outputs the values in ascending order. Post-order: left, right, node — used to delete a tree safely and to produce postfix (Reverse Polish) notation. A binary search tree keeps smaller values left and larger right, giving O(log n) search when reasonably balanced and O(n) when badly unbalanced.","reforge":{"stem":"Searching a binary search tree degrades to O(n) when the tree:","options":{"A":"Contains only integer values.","B":"Is traversed in post-order rather than in-order.","C":"Becomes unbalanced, resembling a list","D":"Holds more than 1000 nodes in total."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-13","stem":"A linked list stores each item together with:","options":{"A":"A complete copy of the entire list.","B":"A pointer to the next node","C":"Its position expressed as an array index.","D":"A checksum used to verify the data."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Each node holds data plus a pointer to the next node; the last points to null. Advantages over an array: size can grow and shrink dynamically, and inserting or deleting requires only pointer changes rather than shifting elements. Disadvantages: no direct indexed access, so finding the nth item means traversing from the start, and extra memory is used for pointers. A doubly linked list adds a backward pointer, allowing traversal in both directions.","reforge":{"stem":"Inserting an item into the middle of a linked list is usually faster than into an array because:","options":{"A":"Linked lists are always stored in contiguous memory.","B":"Arrays must be sorted before any insertion.","C":"Linked lists automatically compress their data.","D":"Only two pointers change, with no elements shifted"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N1-14","stem":"In object-oriented programming, encapsulation refers to:","options":{"A":"Creating a new class that inherits from an existing one.","B":"Allowing one method name to behave differently in different classes.","C":"Bundling data with its methods and restricting direct access","D":"Producing multiple instances of a class at runtime."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-OOP","scaffold":"Encapsulation: attributes are made private and accessed through public methods (getters and setters), so an object controls its own state and validation cannot be bypassed. Inheritance: a subclass acquires the attributes and methods of a superclass, and may extend or override them. Polymorphism: the same method call behaves differently depending on the object's class. Abstraction: exposing only essential features and hiding implementation detail.","reforge":{"stem":"A Dog class and a Cat class each provide their own speak() method, and calling speak() on either gives the appropriate sound. This demonstrates:","options":{"A":"Polymorphism, as the same call differs per class","B":"Encapsulation, since the method data is private.","C":"Instantiation, since objects are created from classes.","D":"Aggregation, since the classes are combined."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-15","stem":"Making a class attribute private rather than public mainly ensures that:","options":{"A":"The attribute occupies less memory when the object is created.","B":"Subclasses are prevented from inheriting the class at all.","C":"The attribute is shared by every instance of the class.","D":"It can only change through methods that validate the value"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-OOP","scaffold":"Private attributes cannot be read or written directly from outside the class; access goes through public methods. This lets the class enforce validation (rejecting a negative age, for example), change its internal representation without breaking other code, and keep invariants true. It is the practical benefit of encapsulation. Note private does not mean the attribute is unique per object — that is the difference between instance and class (static) attributes.","reforge":{"stem":"A setter method rejects any age below zero before storing it. This illustrates the benefit of:","options":{"A":"Inheritance, because behaviour is reused.","B":"Encapsulation, as the class controls its state","C":"Instantiation, because objects are created.","D":"Overloading, because the method has several forms."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-16","stem":"A subclass overriding a method inherited from its superclass means the subclass:","options":{"A":"Provides its own implementation of that method","B":"Deletes the method from the superclass permanently.","C":"Calls the superclass method twice in succession.","D":"Prevents any further subclasses being created."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-OOP","scaffold":"Overriding: a subclass redefines an inherited method with the same name and signature, so the subclass version runs for objects of that type — the mechanism behind polymorphism. Overloading (a different idea) is several methods sharing a name but differing in parameters, resolved at compile time. Inheritance models an 'is-a' relationship; composition, where one object contains another, models 'has-a' and is often more flexible.","reforge":{"stem":"A Car class contains an Engine object as one of its attributes. This relationship is best described as:","options":{"A":"Inheritance, because a Car is a kind of Engine.","B":"Polymorphism, because Engine behaves differently.","C":"Composition, because a Car has an Engine","D":"Overriding, because Car redefines Engine's methods."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-17","stem":"The denary number 45 is represented in 8-bit binary as:","options":{"A":"00101100","B":"00101101","C":"01011010","D":"00110101"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Place values in 8-bit binary are 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. For 45: 32 fits leaving 13, 8 fits leaving 5, 4 fits leaving 1, then 1 fits leaving 0 — so 32+8+4+1, giving 00101101. Check by adding the set bits back. A useful sanity test is that an odd number must end in 1 and an even number in 0, which immediately rules out several distractors.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the denary value of the 8-bit binary number 00011010?","options":{"A":"52, from adding 32, 16 and 4.","B":"13, from adding 8, 4 and 1.","C":"20, from adding 16 and 4.","D":"26, from adding 16, 8 and 2"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N1-18","stem":"Two's complement is used to represent negative integers because it:","options":{"A":"Removes the need to store a sign bit at all.","B":"Doubles the range of positive values that can be stored.","C":"Allows subtraction to be done by binary addition","D":"Stores the magnitude and sign in two separate bytes."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"To negate a number in two's complement, invert all bits and add 1. The most significant bit indicates sign (1 = negative), and an 8-bit range is −128 to +127. Its advantage over sign-and-magnitude is that there is only one representation of zero and that subtraction becomes addition of the negative, so the same adder circuit handles both — simplifying the ALU considerably.","reforge":{"stem":"In 8-bit two's complement, what is the representation of −5?","options":{"A":"11111011, by inverting 5 and adding 1","B":"11111010, by inverting the bits of 5 only.","C":"10000101, using a sign bit with the magnitude.","D":"00000101, since the sign is stored separately."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N1-19","stem":"Hexadecimal is often used by programmers to represent binary because it:","options":{"A":"Can store larger numbers than binary in the same space.","B":"Is the format processors use internally for arithmetic.","C":"Removes the need for any binary conversion.","D":"Is compact, each digit mapping to four bits"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"One hex digit represents exactly four bits, so a byte is two hex digits — 11111010 becomes FA. This makes long binary values shorter to write and much easier to read and check, reducing transcription errors in memory addresses, colour codes and MAC addresses. Hex is purely a human convenience: the machine still stores binary, and no information is added or removed by the notation.","reforge":{"stem":"The binary value 10110110 written in hexadecimal is:","options":{"A":"6B, since the nibbles are read right to left.","B":"B6, since 1011 is B and 0110 is 6","C":"A6, since 1011 is A and 0110 is 6.","D":"B9, since 0110 is 9."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N1-20","stem":"Increasing the sample rate when recording digital audio will:","options":{"A":"Capture higher frequencies, increasing file size","B":"Reduce the file size while improving the sound quality.","C":"Increase the number of bits used for each individual sample.","D":"Have no effect unless the bit depth is also reduced."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Sample rate (Hz) is how many times per second the amplitude is measured; higher rates capture higher frequencies more faithfully. Bit depth is how many bits record each sample; more bits give finer amplitude resolution and less quantisation noise. File size ≈ sample rate × bit depth × duration × channels. Both improve fidelity and both increase size — keep the two ideas distinct, as questions often swap them deliberately.","reforge":{"stem":"An audio file is 44,100 Hz, 16-bit, mono, lasting 10 seconds. Which change would halve the file size?","options":{"A":"Doubling the sample rate to 88,200 Hz.","B":"Recording in stereo instead of mono.","C":"Reducing the bit depth to 8 bits","D":"Increasing the duration to 20 seconds."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N1-21","stem":"Lossless compression differs from lossy compression because lossless compression:","options":{"A":"Always produces a smaller file than lossy compression does.","B":"Allows the original data to be reconstructed exactly","C":"Can only be applied to text files and never to images.","D":"Discards data judged imperceptible to the user."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Lossless (ZIP, PNG, FLAC, run-length encoding, Huffman coding) removes redundancy so the original is restored bit for bit — essential for text, code and archives. Lossy (JPEG, MP3) permanently discards detail the eye or ear is less sensitive to, giving much smaller files at the cost of fidelity, and repeated re-encoding degrades quality further. The choice depends on whether exact reconstruction matters, not on which compresses more.","reforge":{"stem":"Run-length encoding would compress which image most effectively?","options":{"A":"A photograph with continuous gradients of colour.","B":"An image already compressed using JPEG.","C":"A heavily textured image with random pixel noise.","D":"A logo with large blocks of one colour"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N1-22","stem":"An ASCII character set using 7 bits can represent how many distinct characters?","options":{"A":"256, calculated as 2⁸.","B":"127, since one code is reserved.","C":"128, calculated as 2⁷","D":"49, calculated as 7²."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"n bits give 2ⁿ distinct combinations, so 7-bit ASCII gives 128 codes (0–127) covering English letters, digits, punctuation and control characters. Extended ASCII uses 8 bits for 256. Unicode uses more bits — UTF-8 is variable-length, from one byte for ASCII-compatible characters up to four — allowing over a million code points so that most of the world's writing systems and emoji can be represented.","reforge":{"stem":"Unicode was introduced mainly to overcome which limitation of ASCII?","options":{"A":"ASCII could not represent non-English writing systems","B":"ASCII stored characters as images rather than codes.","C":"ASCII required more storage per character than Unicode.","D":"ASCII files could not be compressed effectively."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N5-01","stem":"In most languages, the expression \"7\" + 3 causes an error or unexpected output because:","options":{"A":"Integers cannot appear on the right of an operator.","B":"Addition is not defined for any numeric values.","C":"Quotation marks always denote a comment line.","D":"The string and the integer are different data types"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"\"7\" is a string; 3 is an integer. Some languages raise a type error, others silently concatenate or coerce, which hides the bug. Casting converts deliberately: int(\"7\") gives 7, str(3) gives \"3\". Statically typed languages check types at compile time and catch this early; dynamically typed ones check at runtime, so the error surfaces only when that line executes — a reason to validate and cast input read from a keyboard or file.","reforge":{"stem":"Input read from a keyboard is usually returned as a string, so before arithmetic it must be:","options":{"A":"Stored in a global rather than a local variable.","B":"Cast to a numeric type such as integer","C":"Written to a file and then read back.","D":"Compared with a constant of the same name."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.1"},{"id":"CS-N5-02","stem":"In a two-dimensional array declared as scores[row][column], the first index usually selects the:","options":{"A":"Row; the second selects the column","B":"Data type stored at that position.","C":"Total number of elements in the array.","D":"Memory address where the array begins."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"A two-dimensional array is an array of arrays: the first index selects the row, the second the element within it. Nested loops traverse it, the outer over rows and the inner over columns. It suits grids — a seating plan, a game board, a table of results. Off-by-one errors are common at the boundaries, so check whether indices start at 0 and whether the upper limit is inclusive.","reforge":{"stem":"Traversing every element of a two-dimensional array normally requires:","options":{"A":"A recursive call for each individual element.","B":"A single loop running the length of one row.","C":"Two nested loops, for rows and columns","D":"A stack holding the address of every element."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N5-03","stem":"Declaring a value as a constant rather than a variable means it:","options":{"A":"Occupies no memory while the program runs.","B":"Cannot be changed while the program runs","C":"Is stored in secondary storage automatically.","D":"Is visible only inside the current subroutine."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"A constant is assigned once and cannot be reassigned, so an accidental write is caught rather than silently corrupting the value. Using named constants such as VAT_RATE also makes code self-documenting and means a change to the rate is made in one place rather than scattered through the program. This is separate from scope, which governs where a name is visible, and from data type, which governs what values it may hold.","reforge":{"stem":"Replacing the literal 0.20 throughout a program with a named constant VAT_RATE mainly improves:","options":{"A":"Security, since the value becomes encrypted.","B":"Execution speed, since constants are read faster.","C":"Memory use, since the value is stored once only.","D":"Maintainability, since one edit changes every use"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"}]},"CS-2":{"label":"Data Structures & Networks","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"CS-09","stem":"A stack data structure operates on which principle?","options":{"A":"Last In, First Out — the most recently added item is removed first.","B":"First In, First Out — the first item added is the first removed.","C":"Random access — any element can be accessed directly by index.","D":"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Stack: LIFO (Last In, First Out). Think of a stack of plates — you add (push) and remove (pop) from the top only. Operations: push (add to top), pop (remove from top), peek (view top without removing), isEmpty. Used in: call stack (function calls), undo functionality, expression evaluation, depth-first search. Contrasted with a queue (FIFO — first in, first out), which adds at the back and removes from the front.","reforge":{"stem":"Which real-world scenario is best modelled by a stack?","options":{"A":"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm.","B":"A hospital waiting list — patients are seen in order of arrival.","C":"A browser's back button — the most recently visited page is the first returned to when you press back.","D":"A playlist — songs play in the order they were added."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-10","stem":"In a binary tree, what is the maximum number of nodes at depth d (where the root is at depth 0)?","options":{"A":"d","B":"2^d","C":"There is no difference.","D":"2d"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Binary tree: each node has at most 2 children (left and right). At depth 0: 1 node (root = 2⁰). At depth 1: up to 2 nodes (2¹). At depth 2: up to 4 nodes (2²). At depth d: up to 2^d nodes. A perfect binary tree of height h has 2^(h+1) − 1 total nodes. This exponential growth is what makes binary search efficient — each level doubles the number of nodes.","reforge":{"stem":"A binary search tree has height 4 (root at height 0). What is the maximum total number of nodes it can contain?","options":{"A":"16 nodes — 2⁴ = 16 (height 4, leaf nodes only).","B":"8 nodes — 2³, which would be height 3.","C":"BFS uses less memory than DFS in all cases because it doesn't use a stack.","D":"31 nodes — using 2^(h+1) − 1 where h = 4 gives 2⁵ − 1 = 31 total nodes."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-11","stem":"The OSI model layer responsible for logical addressing (IP addresses) and routing packets between networks is:","options":{"A":"Layer 1 — Physical","B":"Layer 2 — Data Link","C":"Layer 3 — Network","D":"Layer 4 — Transport"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"OSI model (7 layers, bottom to top): Physical (bits/cables), Data Link (MAC addresses, frames, switches), Network (IP addresses, routing, routers), Transport (TCP/UDP, port numbers, end-to-end delivery), Session, Presentation, Application. The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing — IP addresses — and routing between different networks. Data Link (Layer 2) handles physical addressing within the same network using MAC addresses.","reforge":{"stem":"A packet travels from a computer in London to a server in Tokyo. Which OSI layer handles the routing decisions along the way?","options":{"A":"Layer 3 — Network, using IP addresses to make routing decisions at each router hop.","B":"Layer 4 — Transport, using port numbers.","C":"One central server for every service, a client-server architecture description unrelated to which OSI layer handles packet routing decisions.","D":"Layer 2 — Data Link, using MAC addresses."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-12","stem":"TCP differs from UDP primarily in that TCP:","options":{"A":"Operates at the Network layer rather than the Transport layer.","B":"Is faster because it sends data without waiting for acknowledgement.","C":"Can only be used for video streaming applications.","D":"Provides reliable, ordered delivery with acknowledgements"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol): connection-oriented (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), reliable (acknowledgements + retransmission), ordered (sequence numbers), flow controlled. Used for: web (HTTP/HTTPS), email, file transfer. UDP (User Datagram Protocol): connectionless, no acknowledgements, no ordering, no retransmission — faster but unreliable. Used for: video streaming, online gaming, DNS, VoIP — where speed matters more than perfect delivery.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is UDP preferred over TCP for live video streaming?","options":{"A":"UDP provides better data security than TCP for video streams.","B":"UDP is faster — it sends packets without acknowledgements or retransmission, accepting some data loss. Streaming tolerates this; file transfers cannot.","C":"TCP cannot handle the video file formats used in live streaming.","D":"Increase a monitor's resolution, a display-hardware claim that has nothing to do with why UDP is generally preferred over TCP for live video streaming specifically."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-13","stem":"A queue implemented using an array has a 'rear' pointer at index 7 in an array of size 8. What problem does a circular queue solve?","options":{"A":"It reuses vacated front positions by wrapping the rear pointer back to index 0 — preventing false 'full' conditions when space exists at the front of the array.","B":"Only a fixed numeric index, a description that does not explain what practical problem a circular queue's wraparound design of the rear pointer is actually intended to solve.","C":"It enables priority-based removal of elements.","D":"It allows the queue to hold more than 8 elements."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Linear queue problem: after dequeue operations, front pointer advances but those array positions cannot be reused without shifting all elements (O(n)). When rear reaches the end, the array appears full even if spaces exist at the front. Circular queue: uses modular arithmetic ((rear + 1) % size) to wrap around — rear pointer goes 7 → 0 → 1, reusing freed positions. Full condition: (rear + 1) % size == front. More efficient for fixed-size queues.","reforge":{"stem":"How does a circular queue detect whether it is full?","options":{"A":"When the rear pointer equals the array size.","B":"When all elements have the same value.","C":"When (rear + 1) % size == front","D":"When the front pointer reaches index 0."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-14","stem":"Public key cryptography (asymmetric encryption) works because:","options":{"A":"A reversible compression method that always restores the original input exactly, a description of lossless compression unrelated to how public key cryptography actually works.","B":"The receiver's public key encrypts the message, but only the receiver's mathematically linked private key can decrypt it — the public key cannot reverse its own encryption.","C":"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm.","D":"A trusted third party holds the key and decrypts messages on both sides."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Asymmetric encryption: key pair — public key (shared openly) and private key (kept secret). Anyone can encrypt with the public key; only the private key holder can decrypt. Based on mathematical problems that are easy to compute in one direction but infeasible to reverse (e.g. multiplying two large primes is easy; factoring their product is computationally infeasible). Used in HTTPS (TLS), SSH, digital signatures. Solves the key distribution problem of symmetric encryption.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does public key cryptography solve a problem that symmetric encryption cannot?","options":{"A":"Two usernames sharing the same password, an authentication weakness that has nothing whatsoever to do with the key-exchange problem public key cryptography was actually designed to solve.","B":"Symmetric encryption cannot physically be used for internet-based communication.","C":"Public keys are longer and therefore provide stronger encryption by default.","D":"Symmetric encryption requires sharing a secret key in advance — a problem over public networks. Asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, avoiding this key-exchange vulnerability."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-15","stem":"In a graph, a depth-first search (DFS) differs from breadth-first search (BFS) in that DFS:","options":{"A":"Always finds the shortest path between two nodes.","B":"Uses a queue to track which nodes to visit next.","C":"Explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking, using a stack (or recursion).","D":"Messages are compressed before transmission, making them unreadable without the decompression algorithm."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"DFS: goes deep first — follows a path until it reaches a dead end, then backtracks. Uses a stack (LIFO) or recursion. Suitable for: maze solving, topological sort, cycle detection. BFS: explores all neighbours at the current depth before going deeper. Uses a queue (FIFO). Finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. Memory: DFS uses O(depth) space; BFS uses O(width) space — BFS can use much more memory on wide graphs.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is BFS preferred over DFS for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph?","options":{"A":"BFS explores level by level, so it finds the shortest path","B":"BFS is always faster than DFS for finding any path in a graph.","C":"DFS cannot traverse graphs that contain cycles.","D":"BFS uses less memory than DFS in all cases because it doesn't use a stack."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-16","stem":"Normalisation in relational databases aims to:","options":{"A":"Sort columns alphabetically only, a cosmetic reordering unrelated to what normalisation in relational databases is actually intended to achieve.","B":"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.","C":"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format.","D":"Reduce data redundancy and update anomalies by organising data into well-structured tables with clear dependencies."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Normalisation: process of structuring a relational database to reduce redundancy and dependency. Key normal forms: 1NF (atomic values, no repeating groups); 2NF (no partial dependencies — non-key attributes depend on the whole primary key); 3NF (no transitive dependencies — non-key attributes depend only on the primary key). Benefits: eliminates update anomalies (changing one row inconsistently), insertion anomalies, deletion anomalies. Trade-off: more joins required for queries.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an update anomaly, and how does normalisation prevent it?","options":{"A":"An error caused by using incorrect SQL syntax in a query.","B":"An update anomaly — duplicated data in a non-normalised table means one fact must be changed in multiple rows; missing one creates inconsistency.","C":"A security vulnerability created by storing unencrypted sensitive data.","D":"Remove every relationship from a database, leaving separate records with no links between related entities, the opposite of what normalisation and update anomalies are about."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-01","spec":"CS-2","stem":"The main purpose of a protocol is to:","options":{"A":"define agreed rules for communication","B":"It encrypts all data sent over the internet.","C":"store a password in plain text","D":"replace every network device"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Protocols allow different systems to communicate predictably.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: The main purpose of a protocol is to:","options":{"A":"define agreed rules for communication","B":"The link layer, which handles transmission over the physical medium.","C":"The transport layer, which splits data into segments and checks delivery.","D":"Sends data without establishing a connection first."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-02","spec":"CS-2","stem":"DNS translates:","options":{"A":"It provides wireless connectivity to devices.","B":"domain names into IP addresses","C":"binary into analogue sound","D":"web pages into machine code"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"DNS lets users use memorable names while networks route using addresses.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: DNS translates:","options":{"A":"Reserves a dedicated path for the whole conversation.","B":"domain names into IP addresses","C":"Can only be used across a single local network.","D":"Requires all data to travel by an identical route."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-03","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A router primarily forwards packets using:","options":{"A":"MAC addresses only within one application","B":"file extensions","C":"IP addresses and routing information","D":"database primary keys"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Routers choose paths between networks using network-layer information.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A router primarily forwards packets using:","options":{"A":"Was compressed before it was transmitted.","B":"Reversible, so the original password can be recovered.","C":"IP addresses and routing information","D":"Compressible, so hashes take little storage."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-04","spec":"CS-2","stem":"TCP provides:","options":{"A":"Operates at the Network layer rather than the Transport layer.","B":"encryption without keys","C":"domain-name registration","D":"reliable, ordered delivery with error and flow control"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"TCP can retransmit lost data and maintain order.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: TCP provides:","options":{"A":"UDP provides better data security than TCP for video streams.","B":"No foreign keys referring to any other table.","C":"SELECT * FROM Students ORDER BY Mark > 70;","D":"reliable, ordered delivery with error and flow control"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-05","spec":"CS-2","stem":"UDP may be preferred for live streaming because it:","options":{"A":"avoids retransmission delays when low latency matters","B":"guarantees every packet arrives","C":"TCP cannot handle the video file formats used in live streaming.","D":"requires a three-way handshake"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Applications may tolerate occasional loss to preserve smooth timing.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: UDP may be preferred for live streaming because it:","options":{"A":"avoids retransmission delays when low latency matters","B":"Number of devices that may be assigned an IP address.","C":"Physical distance the signal can travel unaided.","D":"Security of the data while it is in transit."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-06","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A MAC address identifies:","options":{"A":"a web page's domain name","B":"a network interface at the local network level","C":"UDP is required by law for licensed video content.","D":"a CPU instruction"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"MAC addresses are hardware-level identifiers used on local networks.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A MAC address identifies:","options":{"A":"The order in which to visit every node exactly once.","B":"a network interface at the local network level","C":"Recursion with no additional data structure.","D":"A binary search tree of visited nodes."},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-07","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A firewall is used to:","options":{"A":"compress every image","B":"replace a backup system","C":"filter network traffic according to rules","D":"The algorithm will always run more quickly"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Firewalls can block unauthorised connections but are not complete security.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A firewall is used to:","options":{"A":"Assigning each device the same private address.","B":"Compressing the packet headers before sending.","C":"filter network traffic according to rules","D":"Every file held on the system each time."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-08","spec":"CS-2","stem":"Phishing is an attack that:","options":{"A":"Prevents every cyberattack, an impossible absolute claim unrelated to what phishing actually is as an attack technique.","B":"changes a file into binary","C":"improves password strength","D":"tricks users into revealing information through a deceptive message or site"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Phishing exploits human trust rather than a hardware fault.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: Phishing is an attack that:","options":{"A":"The algorithm will always run more quickly","B":"Asymmetric encryption is mathematically unbreakable; symmetric encryption is not.","C":"The camera will stop recording images","D":"tricks users into revealing information through a deceptive message or site"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-09","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A digital signature helps provide:","options":{"A":"authentication and integrity of a message","B":"It is correct — encrypted data cannot be read.","C":"anonymous authorship only","D":"unlimited storage capacity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"The sender signs with a private key and others verify with the public key.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A digital signature helps provide:","options":{"A":"authentication and integrity of a message","B":"To remove all responsibility for complying with licence terms","C":"The device has insufficient storage capacity","D":"The processor cannot execute machine code"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-10","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A primary key in a relational database:","options":{"A":"must contain duplicate values","B":"uniquely identifies each record","C":"stores every table in one field","D":"is always a foreign key"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Primary keys support identification and relationships between tables.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: A primary key in a relational database:","options":{"A":"To eliminate cybersecurity training","B":"uniquely identifies each record","C":"To guarantee that misuse cannot occur","D":"A new system must be adopted immediately"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-11","spec":"CS-2","stem":"Referential integrity helps ensure that:","options":{"A":"every field contains text","B":"all records are duplicated","C":"a foreign key refers to a valid related record","D":"Speed up database queries by duplicating frequently accessed data."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"It prevents orphaned relationships between related tables.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: Referential integrity helps ensure that:","options":{"A":"First In, First Out — the first item added is the first removed.","B":"Random access — any element can be accessed directly by index.","C":"a foreign key refers to a valid related record","D":"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-12","spec":"CS-2","stem":"Normalisation aims to:","options":{"A":"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.","B":"remove all relationships","C":"increase repeated data","D":"reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Normalised design separates related facts into suitable tables.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Normalisation aims to:","options":{"A":"An error caused by using incorrect SQL syntax in a query.","B":"It sorts elements as they are added.","C":"It enables priority-based removal of elements.","D":"reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-13","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A SQL WHERE clause is used to:","options":{"A":"filter rows before they are returned","B":"A security vulnerability created by storing unencrypted sensitive data.","C":"create a password hash","D":"join a network"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"WHERE applies a condition to selected rows.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A SQL WHERE clause is used to:","options":{"A":"filter rows before they are returned","B":"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.","C":"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format.","D":"increase a monitor's resolution"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-14","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A primary advantage of cloud storage is:","options":{"A":"guaranteed access without a network","B":"remote access and scalable capacity","C":"no provider security risk","D":"permanent offline availability"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"Cloud services offer flexibility but create dependence on connectivity and providers.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A primary advantage of cloud storage is:","options":{"A":"Only the user's email content, a narrow and unrelated claim rather than the actual primary advantage cloud storage provides.","B":"remote access and scalable capacity","C":"encryption without keys","D":"domain-name registration"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-15","spec":"CS-2","stem":"An operating system manages:","options":{"A":"A user to bypass authentication, a security-vulnerability description unrelated to what an operating system actually manages.","B":"a country's exchange rate","C":"hardware resources and services for applications","D":"the content of every website"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"Operating systems schedule processes, manage memory and provide interfaces.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: An operating system manages:","options":{"A":"A network cable as a keyboard, a nonsensical hardware substitution unrelated to what an operating system actually manages.","B":"Virtual machines cannot be connected to a network.","C":"hardware resources and services for applications","D":"changes a file into binary"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2A-16","spec":"CS-2","stem":"Virtual memory uses:","options":{"A":"a faster CPU register only","B":"A user to bypass authentication, a security-vulnerability description unrelated to what virtual memory actually uses to extend apparent main memory.","C":"encryption keys as RAM","D":"secondary storage to extend apparent main memory"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"It allows programs to run when physical RAM is insufficient, but is slower.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: Virtual memory uses:","options":{"A":"all records are duplicated","B":"queries never need conditions","C":"The content of every website, an unrelated claim about web data rather than what virtual memory actually uses to extend apparent main memory.","D":"secondary storage to extend apparent main memory"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2A-17","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A device driver allows:","options":{"A":"the operating system to communicate with particular hardware","B":"Guaranteed access without a network, a claim unrelated to what a device driver actually allows the operating system to do.","C":"a database to remove keys","D":"a compiler to skip syntax"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"Drivers translate operating-system requests for a device.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A device driver allows:","options":{"A":"the operating system to communicate with particular hardware","B":"A user to bypass authentication, a security-vulnerability description unrelated to what a device driver actually allows the operating system to do.","C":"a country's exchange rate","D":"the content of every website"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2A-18","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A lossless compression method:","options":{"A":"Doubles the range of positive values that can be stored.","B":"allows the original data to be reconstructed exactly","C":"removes all metadata permanently","D":"works only on video"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Lossless methods preserve information, which is important for text and executable files.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A lossless compression method:","options":{"A":"Can store larger numbers than binary in the same space.","B":"allows the original data to be reconstructed exactly","C":"two copies of a file","D":"two public IP addresses"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2A-19","spec":"CS-2","stem":"Two-factor authentication improves security by requiring:","options":{"A":"Asymmetric encryption is illegal for commercial use.","B":"two copies of a file","C":"two independent forms of evidence of identity","D":"two public IP addresses"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Combining factors reduces the effect of a stolen password.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: Two-factor authentication improves security by requiring:","options":{"A":"The link layer, which handles transmission over the physical medium.","B":"The transport layer, which splits data into segments and checks delivery.","C":"two independent forms of evidence of identity","D":"Sends data without establishing a connection first."},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-20","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A denial-of-service attack attempts to:","options":{"A":"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority.","B":"repair corrupted data","C":"reduce network traffic","D":"overwhelm a service so legitimate users cannot access it"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Distributed attacks can use many compromised devices at once.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A denial-of-service attack attempts to:","options":{"A":"A printer queue — documents print in the order they were submitted.","B":"Can only be used across a single local network.","C":"Requires all data to travel by an identical route.","D":"overwhelm a service so legitimate users cannot access it"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2A-21","spec":"CS-2","stem":"A checksum is used to:","options":{"A":"detect accidental changes in transmitted or stored data","B":"prove who wrote a message","C":"create a new IP address","D":"Encrypting every file stored on the connected computers."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"A calculated value can reveal that data has changed, although it is not strong authentication.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A checksum is used to:","options":{"A":"detect accidental changes in transmitted or stored data","B":"Was compressed before it was transmitted.","C":"A hospital waiting list — patients are seen in order of arrival.","D":"Compressible, so hashes take little storage."},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N2-01","stem":"In the TCP/IP model, which layer is responsible for routing packets between networks?","options":{"A":"The application layer, which formats data for the user program.","B":"The internet layer, which addresses and routes packets","C":"The link layer, which handles transmission over the physical medium.","D":"The transport layer, which splits data into segments and checks delivery."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"TCP/IP has four layers. Application (HTTP, FTP, SMTP) — produces the data. Transport (TCP, UDP) — splits it into segments, adds port numbers, and for TCP handles acknowledgement and reordering. Internet (IP) — adds source and destination IP addresses and routes packets across networks. Link — frames the data for the physical medium and uses MAC addresses. Each layer adds its own header, and the receiving stack strips them in reverse.","reforge":{"stem":"Layering a protocol stack is useful mainly because it:","options":{"A":"Makes every protocol run at the same speed.","B":"Guarantees that no packet is ever lost in transit.","C":"Removes the need for addressing at the internet layer.","D":"Lets one layer be changed without rewriting the others"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-02","stem":"TCP is preferred to UDP for transferring a file because TCP:","options":{"A":"Sends data without establishing a connection first.","B":"Has a smaller header, so it uses less bandwidth.","C":"Acknowledges segments and retransmits losses","D":"Delivers segments faster by skipping error checking."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"TCP is connection-oriented: it performs a three-way handshake, numbers segments, acknowledges them, retransmits losses and reassembles in order — reliable but with more overhead and latency. UDP is connectionless: it just sends, with a small header and no acknowledgement or reordering. Files, web pages and email need TCP's reliability; live video, voice and online gaming prefer UDP because a late packet is worse than a missing one.","reforge":{"stem":"Live video calling usually uses UDP rather than TCP because:","options":{"A":"Retransmitting a late packet only adds delay","B":"UDP encrypts the stream automatically.","C":"UDP guarantees every frame arrives in order.","D":"TCP cannot carry audio or video data."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-03","stem":"A subnet mask of 255.255.255.0 means that in each IP address:","options":{"A":"All four octets identify the individual host.","B":"The address is reserved for private use only.","C":"The first octet identifies the network and the rest the host.","D":"The first three octets are the network, the last the host"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"A subnet mask splits an IPv4 address into a network part and a host part. Where a mask octet is 255 (binary 11111111), those bits belong to the network; where it is 0, they identify the host. So 255.255.255.0 (a /24) gives 256 addresses per subnet, of which 254 are usable — the lowest is the network address and the highest is the broadcast address. Routers compare the network part to decide whether traffic stays local or is forwarded.","reforge":{"stem":"How many usable host addresses does a /24 subnet provide?","options":{"A":"256, since eight host bits give 2⁸ addresses.","B":"254, as network and broadcast addresses are reserved","C":"255, because only the broadcast address is reserved.","D":"128, because half the range is reserved for routing."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-04","stem":"The Domain Name System (DNS) exists mainly to:","options":{"A":"Translate human-readable domain names into IP addresses","B":"Assign IP addresses automatically to devices joining a network.","C":"Encrypt traffic between a browser and a web server.","D":"Divide a large network into smaller broadcast domains."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"DNS resolves names like example.com to IP addresses, querying a hierarchy of resolvers, root, top-level and authoritative servers, with results cached to reduce lookups. Distinguish it from DHCP, which leases IP addresses to devices joining a network; NAT, which maps many private addresses to one public one; and TLS, which encrypts traffic. Exam questions often list all four and rely on students confusing DNS with DHCP.","reforge":{"stem":"A laptop joins a home network and is given an IP address automatically. Which protocol did this?","options":{"A":"DNS, because it resolves the network name.","B":"HTTPS, because the connection is secured.","C":"DHCP, which leases addresses to clients","D":"NAT, because the address is private."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-05","stem":"Packet switching differs from circuit switching because packet switching:","options":{"A":"Reserves a dedicated path for the whole conversation.","B":"Sends packets independently by varying routes","C":"Can only be used across a single local network.","D":"Requires all data to travel by an identical route."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Circuit switching reserves a physical path for the duration of a call — guaranteed bandwidth, but the circuit is wasted during silence. Packet switching breaks data into packets, each with a header carrying source, destination and sequence number; routers forward each packet by whatever route is best at that moment, so packets may arrive out of order and are reassembled at the destination. It uses the network far more efficiently and copes with link failure.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does each packet carry a sequence number?","options":{"A":"So the sender knows which port to use.","B":"So routers can encrypt each packet separately.","C":"So the packet can be compressed before sending.","D":"So out-of-order packets can be reassembled"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N2-06","stem":"A firewall protects a network principally by:","options":{"A":"Removing viruses already installed on a workstation.","B":"Encrypting every file stored on the connected computers.","C":"Inspecting traffic and blocking it by rule","D":"Creating backups of data before it leaves the network."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"A firewall filters traffic at the network boundary using rules about IP addresses, ports and protocols, and may inspect packet contents. It cannot remove malware already inside, recover data or replace backups. Layered defence matters: firewalls control traffic, anti-malware detects malicious software, encryption protects data if it is stolen, access control limits what each user can reach, and patching closes known vulnerabilities.","reforge":{"stem":"Encryption protects stolen data because the attacker:","options":{"A":"Cannot read it without the decryption key","B":"Cannot copy the file from the storage device.","C":"Is prevented from connecting to the network.","D":"Triggers an alert whenever the file is opened."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-07","stem":"In symmetric encryption, the same key is used to encrypt and decrypt. The main practical problem this creates is:","options":{"A":"It is far slower than asymmetric encryption.","B":"The ciphertext is always longer than the plaintext.","C":"The key must be sent with the message for decryption to work.","D":"Distributing the key securely to the other party"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Symmetric encryption (AES) is fast and suits bulk data, but both parties need the same secret key, and sending it over an insecure channel exposes it. Asymmetric encryption uses a public key to encrypt and a mathematically related private key to decrypt, solving distribution but running much more slowly. In practice TLS combines them: asymmetric encryption establishes a shared session key, then symmetric encryption protects the actual traffic.","reforge":{"stem":"HTTPS uses asymmetric encryption at the start of a session and symmetric encryption afterwards because this:","options":{"A":"Avoids the need for any certificates.","B":"Solves key distribution, then gets symmetric speed","C":"Makes the connection impossible to intercept.","D":"Allows the server to store the private key publicly."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-08","stem":"A digital signature allows a recipient to verify that a message:","options":{"A":"Came from the stated sender and was not altered","B":"Travelled by the shortest available route.","C":"Cannot be read by anyone other than the recipient.","D":"Was compressed before it was transmitted."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"To sign, the sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with their private key. The recipient decrypts it with the sender's public key and compares against their own hash of the message. A match proves authenticity (only the private key holder could have signed) and integrity (any change alters the hash). Note it does not provide confidentiality — the message itself is not encrypted unless that is done separately.","reforge":{"stem":"A digital certificate issued by a certificate authority mainly confirms that:","options":{"A":"The website's traffic is compressed efficiently.","B":"The site has never suffered a security breach.","C":"A public key belongs to that organisation","D":"The website is free of malware."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N2-09","stem":"A hashing algorithm used for storing passwords should be:","options":{"A":"Reversible, so the original password can be recovered.","B":"One-way, so the stored value cannot be reversed","C":"Compressible, so hashes take little storage.","D":"Fast enough to hash millions of guesses per second."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Hashing is deliberately one-way: it maps input to a fixed-length digest that cannot practically be reversed, so a stolen database does not reveal passwords. Verification re-hashes the entered password and compares digests. Good password hashing is deliberately slow to resist brute force, and adds a random salt per user so identical passwords give different hashes and precomputed rainbow tables fail. Encryption, by contrast, is designed to be reversed with a key.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding a random salt to each password before hashing prevents:","options":{"A":"Users from choosing weak or common passwords.","B":"Administrators from resetting a forgotten password.","C":"The hash function from ever producing a collision.","D":"Precomputed rainbow tables from matching the hashes"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-10","stem":"A phishing attack differs from most other attacks because it primarily exploits:","options":{"A":"Weak encryption on the wireless access point.","B":"An unpatched flaw in the operating system kernel.","C":"Human trust rather than a technical vulnerability","D":"A buffer overflow in the web server software."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Phishing uses social engineering: a message that appears to come from a trusted source persuades the user to reveal credentials or open an attachment. Because the weakness is human, the defences are training, verifying senders, and multi-factor authentication rather than patching. Contrast with technical attacks: SQL injection exploits unvalidated input, denial of service overwhelms capacity, and brute force tries many keys.","reforge":{"stem":"SQL injection is best prevented by:","options":{"A":"Validating and parameterising input","B":"Using a longer administrator password.","C":"Encrypting the database backups.","D":"Installing a faster web server."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N2-11","stem":"In a relational database, a foreign key is:","options":{"A":"A field that uniquely identifies each record in its own table.","B":"A key encrypted before the database is stored on disk.","C":"An index created to speed up searching a large table.","D":"An attribute referring to another table's primary key"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"A primary key uniquely identifies each record in its table. A foreign key is an attribute in a second table holding the value of that primary key, creating the relationship between them and enforcing referential integrity — you cannot reference a record that does not exist. A composite key uses two or more fields together where no single field is unique, which is how many-to-many relationships are resolved through a linking table.","reforge":{"stem":"A many-to-many relationship between Students and Courses is normally implemented using:","options":{"A":"A single table containing repeated course fields.","B":"A linking table holding both foreign keys","C":"Two tables joined directly by their primary keys.","D":"An index on the student name field."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-12","stem":"A database table is in first normal form when it contains:","options":{"A":"No repeating groups; each field holds one value","B":"No transitive dependency between non-key attributes.","C":"No partial dependency on part of a composite key.","D":"No foreign keys referring to any other table."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"1NF — atomic values only, no repeating groups. 2NF — in 1NF and no partial dependency, so no non-key attribute depends on only part of a composite key. 3NF — in 2NF and no transitive dependency, so no non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute. Normalisation removes redundancy, prevents update, insert and delete anomalies, and saves storage, at the cost of needing joins when querying.","reforge":{"stem":"The main benefit of normalising a database to 3NF is that it:","options":{"A":"Makes every query run faster than before.","B":"Removes the need for any primary keys.","C":"Reduces redundancy and update anomalies","D":"Allows all data to be stored in one table."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-13","stem":"Which SQL statement returns only students whose mark is above 70?","options":{"A":"SELECT * FROM Students ORDER BY Mark > 70;","B":"SELECT * FROM Students WHERE Mark > 70;","C":"SELECT Mark FROM Students GROUP BY 70;","D":"SELECT * FROM Students HAVING Mark;"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"SELECT chooses fields, FROM names the table, WHERE filters individual rows, ORDER BY sorts the result, GROUP BY aggregates rows, and HAVING filters those aggregated groups. The common error is using HAVING where WHERE is needed: WHERE filters before grouping and works on raw rows, HAVING filters after grouping and works on aggregate values such as COUNT or AVG.","reforge":{"stem":"Which clause filters rows after they have been grouped by an aggregate function?","options":{"A":"WHERE, because it always filters rows.","B":"ORDER BY, because it arranges the results.","C":"SELECT, because it chooses the fields.","D":"HAVING, which applies to grouped rows"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-14","stem":"A transaction in a database system is described as atomic. This means it:","options":{"A":"Affects only one record in one table.","B":"Runs faster than any other type of query.","C":"Either completes fully or has no effect","D":"Cannot be rolled back once it has started."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"ACID properties: Atomicity — all operations in a transaction succeed or none do, so a transfer never debits without crediting. Consistency — the database moves from one valid state to another. Isolation — concurrent transactions do not interfere, usually enforced by record locking. Durability — once committed, changes survive a crash, supported by a transaction log used to recover.","reforge":{"stem":"Record locking during a transaction primarily prevents:","options":{"A":"Two users updating the same record simultaneously","B":"The database from exceeding its storage quota.","C":"A user from reading data they lack permission for.","D":"The transaction log from becoming too large."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-15","stem":"Client-server differs from peer-to-peer because in client-server:","options":{"A":"Every device both requests and supplies resources equally.","B":"Devices must all run the same operating system.","C":"No device can act as a client at any time.","D":"Resources are centrally managed by dedicated servers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Client-server centralises storage, security, backup and updates on dedicated servers — easier to manage and secure, but the server is a single point of failure and costs more. Peer-to-peer gives every device equal status, sharing resources directly: cheap, resilient and easy to set up for small networks, but backup and security are inconsistent and performance degrades as peers join and leave. Scale and administration usually decide which is appropriate.","reforge":{"stem":"A drawback of a client-server network compared with peer-to-peer is that:","options":{"A":"Backups must be performed separately on every device.","B":"Server failure can stop many users working at once","C":"Security policies cannot be applied centrally.","D":"It is impossible to share files between users."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-3.2"},{"id":"CS-N2-16","stem":"Increasing the bandwidth of a network connection directly increases the:","options":{"A":"Volume of data that can be transferred per second","B":"Number of devices that may be assigned an IP address.","C":"Physical distance the signal can travel unaided.","D":"Security of the data while it is in transit."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Bandwidth is capacity — how much data can pass per second. Latency is delay — how long a packet takes to arrive, affected by distance, routing hops and congestion. They are independent: a satellite link can have high bandwidth and high latency. Throughput is what is actually achieved, always at or below bandwidth once protocol overhead, errors, contention and the slowest link in the path are taken into account.","reforge":{"stem":"A satellite link has high bandwidth but users report slow response when clicking links. The likely cause is:","options":{"A":"Insufficient bandwidth for the traffic volume.","B":"An incorrectly configured subnet mask.","C":"High latency from the signal's travel distance","D":"Excessive use of packet compression."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-17","stem":"A hash table offers average-case O(1) lookup because the key is:","options":{"A":"Compared with every stored key until a match is found.","B":"Converted by a hash function directly into an index","C":"Stored twice, once in each of two parallel arrays.","D":"Sorted so that binary search can be applied."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"A hash function maps a key to an array index, so an item can be reached without searching — average O(1) for insertion, deletion and lookup. Two keys mapping to the same index is a collision, handled by chaining (a list at each slot) or open addressing (probing for the next free slot). Performance degrades as the load factor rises, and in the worst case, with everything colliding, lookup becomes O(n).","reforge":{"stem":"Two different keys hash to the same index. This is called a:","options":{"A":"Rehash, resolved by sorting the array.","B":"Traversal, resolved by rebalancing the structure.","C":"Overflow, resolved by increasing the key length.","D":"Collision, resolved by chaining or probing"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N2-18","stem":"A graph differs from a tree because a graph may contain:","options":{"A":"A single node designated as the root.","B":"Only one route between any two nodes.","C":"Cycles, and need not be connected","D":"At most two children for each node."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"A tree is a connected acyclic graph with a root, where exactly one path links any two nodes. A general graph has no root, may be disconnected, and may contain cycles; edges may be directed or undirected and carry weights. Graphs are represented by an adjacency matrix (fast lookup, wasteful for sparse graphs) or an adjacency list (compact for sparse graphs). They model road networks, social connections and dependencies.","reforge":{"stem":"An adjacency list is usually preferred to an adjacency matrix when the graph is:","options":{"A":"Sparse, with relatively few edges","B":"Dense, with most possible edges present.","C":"Undirected rather than directed.","D":"Guaranteed to contain no cycles."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N2-19","stem":"Dijkstra's algorithm is used to find:","options":{"A":"Whether a graph contains any cycles.","B":"The minimum number of colours needed for a graph.","C":"The order in which to visit every node exactly once.","D":"The shortest path from one node to the others"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Dijkstra's algorithm finds the lowest-cost path from a start node to all others in a weighted graph with non-negative weights. It keeps a tentative distance for each node, repeatedly selects the unvisited node with the smallest distance, and relaxes its neighbours. A* extends it with a heuristic estimating remaining distance, which usually reaches a specific target faster. Negative edge weights break Dijkstra's assumption and need a different algorithm.","reforge":{"stem":"A* usually finds a route to a specific destination faster than Dijkstra's algorithm because it:","options":{"A":"Ignores the weights attached to the edges.","B":"Uses a heuristic to guide the search","C":"Examines every node in the graph first.","D":"Requires the graph to contain no cycles."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-N2-20","stem":"A breadth-first traversal of a graph is normally implemented using:","options":{"A":"A queue, so nodes are explored level by level","B":"Recursion with no additional data structure.","C":"A binary search tree of visited nodes.","D":"A stack, so the deepest node is explored first."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"Breadth-first search uses a queue: visit a node, enqueue its unvisited neighbours, then dequeue the next — exploring level by level, which finds the fewest-edge path in an unweighted graph. Depth-first search uses a stack (often the call stack via recursion), following one branch as far as possible before backtracking — useful for detecting cycles and topological sorting. Both need a visited set to avoid revisiting nodes in a cyclic graph.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must a graph traversal keep a set of visited nodes?","options":{"A":"To sort the nodes into ascending order.","B":"To calculate the total weight of the edges.","C":"To prevent revisiting nodes and looping","D":"To convert the graph into a binary tree."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N2-21","stem":"Compared with a physical server, a virtual machine allows an organisation to:","options":{"A":"Remove the need for any operating system.","B":"Run several isolated systems on one machine","C":"Increase raw processing speed beyond the hardware.","D":"Guarantee that no hardware failure can occur."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"A hypervisor lets one physical machine host several virtual machines, each with its own operating system, isolated from the others. Benefits: better hardware utilisation, easy snapshots and migration, isolation for testing, and rapid provisioning. Costs: virtualisation overhead means slightly lower performance than bare metal, and the host remains a single point of hardware failure. Cloud computing sells this capacity on demand.","reforge":{"stem":"A drawback of running many virtual machines on a single host is that:","options":{"A":"Virtual machines cannot be connected to a network.","B":"Each machine must run an identical operating system.","C":"Snapshots of a virtual machine cannot be taken.","D":"One hardware failure can affect every hosted system"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N2-22","stem":"An operating system's scheduler exists to:","options":{"A":"Translate high-level source code into machine code.","B":"Store files in directories on secondary storage.","C":"Decide which process uses the processor next","D":"Detect and remove malicious software automatically."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"The scheduler allocates processor time between processes so that a single core appears to run many programs at once. Common policies: round robin gives each process a fixed time slice; shortest job first minimises average waiting time but can starve long jobs; priority scheduling favours important processes and may use ageing to prevent starvation. Other OS roles are memory management, file management, device drivers and user security.","reforge":{"stem":"Round robin scheduling gives each process a fixed time slice mainly to:","options":{"A":"Ensure every process gets a fair share","B":"Complete the shortest jobs before any others.","C":"Guarantee that no process is ever interrupted.","D":"Reduce the amount of memory each process needs."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N5-04","stem":"Network Address Translation allows several devices to share one public IP address by:","options":{"A":"Encrypting the traffic leaving the local network.","B":"Assigning each device the same private address.","C":"Compressing the packet headers before sending.","D":"Rewriting addresses and ports at the router"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"NAT lets a router map many private addresses (10.x, 172.16-31.x, 192.168.x) onto one public address, tracking which internal device and port each connection belongs to so replies return correctly. It conserves the limited IPv4 address space and incidentally hides the internal structure, since unsolicited inbound traffic has no mapping. IPv6's much larger address space removes the original need for it.","reforge":{"stem":"IPv6 was introduced principally to address which limitation of IPv4?","options":{"A":"IPv4 packets could not be routed between networks.","B":"IPv4 provided too few addresses for demand","C":"IPv4 was unable to carry encrypted traffic.","D":"IPv4 headers could not include a checksum."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N5-05","stem":"An incremental backup differs from a full backup because an incremental backup copies:","options":{"A":"Only files changed since the last backup","B":"Every file held on the system each time.","C":"Only files larger than a set threshold size.","D":"The operating system but not any user data."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"A full backup copies everything: slow to make, quick to restore. An incremental copies only what changed since the previous backup of any kind: fast and small, but restoring needs the last full backup plus every incremental since, so one missing set breaks the chain. A differential copies everything changed since the last full backup: larger each day, but restoring needs only two sets. Backups should be tested and stored off-site.","reforge":{"stem":"Restoring from differential backups is simpler than from incremental ones because it needs:","options":{"A":"No full backup to be taken at any point.","B":"Every differential taken since the last full backup.","C":"Only the most recent differential set","D":"A separate backup of the operating system."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N5-06","stem":"A proxy server placed between users and the internet can:","options":{"A":"Increase the physical bandwidth of the connection.","B":"Cache pages and filter requests centrally","C":"Replace the need for any IP addressing.","D":"Guarantee that no traffic is ever encrypted."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"A proxy makes requests on behalf of clients, so it can cache frequently requested pages to cut external traffic and improve response, enforce content filtering and acceptable-use policy, log activity, and hide client addresses from the destination. It is a control and performance measure rather than a security boundary in itself: a firewall still governs which traffic may pass at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Caching at a proxy server improves response time because a repeated request can be answered:","options":{"A":"By reducing the size of the page requested.","B":"By compressing the page before delivery.","C":"Only after the origin server confirms it.","D":"From local storage, not the origin server"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-ETH-01","stem":"Which principle is most directly protected by data protection law?","options":{"A":"Control over personal data use","B":"A firm's right to publish every record","C":"A requirement to store data permanently","D":"A ban on correcting inaccurate records"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Data protection rules regulate the collection, use, accuracy, security and retention of personal information.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: Which principle is most directly protected by data protection law?","options":{"A":"First In, First Out — the first item added is the first removed.","B":"Random access — any element can be accessed directly by index.","C":"Control over personal data use","D":"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-02","stem":"A company trains a facial-recognition system on an unrepresentative dataset. What is the main ethical risk?","options":{"A":"The algorithm will always run more quickly","B":"Unfair outcomes for some groups","C":"The dataset will become public automatically","D":"The camera will stop recording images"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Unrepresentative training data can produce systematic errors for groups that are poorly represented.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A company trains a facial-recognition system on an unrepresentative dataset. What is the main ethical risk?","options":{"A":"Can only be used for video streaming applications.","B":"It sorts elements as they are added.","C":"It enables priority-based removal of elements.","D":"Unfair outcomes for some groups"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-03","stem":"What is the strongest reason for using encryption when transmitting medical data?","options":{"A":"It proves that the data is accurate","B":"It makes the data impossible to delete","C":"It limits reading by an interceptor","D":"It replaces the need for access controls"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Encryption protects confidentiality in transit, although secure authentication and access controls are still needed.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: What is the strongest reason for using encryption when transmitting medical data?","options":{"A":"It limits reading by an interceptor","B":"Encrypt sensitive data to prevent unauthorised access.","C":"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format.","D":"increase a monitor's resolution"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-04","stem":"Which example best illustrates the digital divide?","options":{"A":"A programmer choosing between languages","B":"A database containing duplicate records","C":"A website using a responsive layout","D":"Unequal access to digital services"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"The digital divide is unequal access to digital technologies and the skills or connectivity needed to use them.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: Which example best illustrates the digital divide?","options":{"A":"unreliable broadcast with no sequencing","B":"Unequal access to digital services","C":"encryption without keys","D":"domain-name registration"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-05","stem":"Why might a software developer choose an open-source licence with attribution conditions?","options":{"A":"To allow reuse while requiring credit for the original work","B":"To prevent anybody from viewing the source code","C":"To guarantee that every derivative becomes commercial","D":"To remove all responsibility for complying with licence terms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Open-source licences vary: some permit reuse provided conditions such as attribution or sharing changes are met.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: Why might a software developer choose an open-source licence with attribution conditions?","options":{"A":"generate random passwords only","B":"The link layer, which handles transmission over the physical medium.","C":"To allow reuse while requiring credit for the original work","D":"changes a file into binary"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-06","stem":"A smart speaker records conversations without a clear user choice. Which concern is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The device has insufficient storage capacity","B":"Unclear recording may breach privacy","C":"The processor cannot execute machine code","D":"The network has too much bandwidth"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Privacy concerns arise when personal information is captured without transparency, necessity or meaningful consent.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: A smart speaker records conversations without a clear user choice. Which concern is most relevant?","options":{"A":"all records are duplicated","B":"queries never need conditions","C":"A firm's right to publish every record","D":"Unclear recording may breach privacy"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-07","stem":"Which change would most reduce the environmental impact of a data centre?","options":{"A":"Increasing unnecessary data duplication","B":"Replacing efficient hardware with older hardware","C":"Efficient hardware and low-carbon power","D":"Keeping unused servers running continuously"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Energy-efficient equipment, workload management and lower-carbon power can reduce a data centre's emissions.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: Which change would most reduce the environmental impact of a data centre?","options":{"A":"Efficient hardware and low-carbon power","B":"The dataset will become public automatically","C":"a country's exchange rate","D":"the content of every website"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-08","stem":"Why can automation create a social concern even when it increases productivity?","options":{"A":"Jobs and inequality may change","B":"It makes every worker's skills identical","C":"It prevents organisations from collecting data","D":"It guarantees decisions are free from bias"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Automation can displace some roles while creating others, so distributional effects and retraining matter.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Why can automation create a social concern even when it increases productivity?","options":{"A":"works only on video","B":"Jobs and inequality may change","C":"two usernames with the same password","D":"two copies of a file"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-09","stem":"What is the purpose of an acceptable-use policy in an organisation?","options":{"A":"To make all users administrators","B":"To set permitted system use","C":"To eliminate cybersecurity training","D":"To guarantee that misuse cannot occur"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"An acceptable-use policy sets behavioural boundaries and responsibilities for using organisational systems.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: What is the purpose of an acceptable-use policy in an organisation?","options":{"A":"The link layer, which handles transmission over the physical medium.","B":"The transport layer, which splits data into segments and checks delivery.","C":"To set permitted system use","D":"Sends data without establishing a connection first."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-10","stem":"What is the most accurate description of the precautionary principle in technology policy?","options":{"A":"A new system must be adopted immediately","B":"Early safeguards for serious risk","C":"Financial costs are the only concern","D":"Technology creates no unintended effects"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"The precautionary principle supports proportionate safeguards where credible risks of serious harm remain uncertain.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: What is the most accurate description of the precautionary principle in technology policy?","options":{"A":"Reserves a dedicated path for the whole conversation.","B":"Can only be used across a single local network.","C":"Requires all data to travel by an identical route.","D":"Early safeguards for serious risk"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-11","stem":"Why is algorithmic transparency important in a high-stakes decision system?","options":{"A":"It supports challenge and accountability","B":"It ensures the algorithm has no mathematical operations","C":"It means source code can never be protected","D":"It removes the need to test the system"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Transparency supports accountability, although explaining a system does not by itself prove that its outcomes are fair.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: Why is algorithmic transparency important in a high-stakes decision system?","options":{"A":"It supports challenge and accountability","B":"Was compressed before it was transmitted.","C":"Reversible, so the original password can be recovered.","D":"Compressible, so hashes take little storage."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"CS-ETH-12","stem":"A school uses monitoring software that flags students as suspicious. What should be considered before deployment?","options":{"A":"Only whether the interface is attractive","B":"Accuracy, privacy and false positives","C":"Whether every alert proves wrongdoing","D":"Whether students can be denied explanations"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"High-impact monitoring needs evidence of accuracy, proportionate use, privacy safeguards and a process to challenge errors.","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5","spec":"CS-2.5","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: A school uses monitoring software that flags students as suspicious. What should be considered before deployment?","options":{"A":"No partial dependency on part of a composite key.","B":"Accuracy, privacy and false positives","C":"No foreign keys referring to any other table.","D":"SELECT * FROM Students ORDER BY Mark > 70;"},"correct":"B"}}]},"CS-3":{"label":"Component 1 — Algorithms, Programming & Systems","color":"#0f766e","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","questions":[{"id":"CS-C1B-01","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A Boolean AND returns true when:","options":{"A":"all its inputs are true","B":"at least one input is true","C":"all inputs are false","D":"the inputs differ"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"AND requires every condition to be true.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A Boolean AND returns true when:","options":{"A":"all its inputs are true","B":"The computer's RAM is insufficient.","C":"A OR B","D":"A AND B"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-02","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"De Morgan's law states that NOT (A AND B) equals:","options":{"A":"(NOT A) AND (NOT B)","B":"(NOT A) OR (NOT B)","C":"A OR B","D":"A AND B"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Negating an AND changes it to OR and negates each input.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: De Morgan's law states that NOT (A AND B) equals:","options":{"A":"the size of the hard drive","B":"(NOT A) OR (NOT B)","C":"SQL into HTML","D":"binary into decimal only"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-03","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A register is:","options":{"A":"Stores data permanently when the power is switched off.","B":"a network protocol","C":"a small, fast storage location inside the CPU","D":"a programming language"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Registers hold values and instructions currently being processed.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A register is:","options":{"A":"used only for backups","B":"Increases the total amount of addressable main memory.","C":"a small, fast storage location inside the CPU","D":"only decimal fractions"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C1B-04","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"The program counter stores:","options":{"A":"the number of users with admin rights","B":"the current user password","C":"the size of the hard drive","D":"the address of the next instruction"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"The fetch-decode-execute cycle updates the program counter.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: The program counter stores:","options":{"A":"stored per pixel","B":"sent per IP address","C":"Allows several processes to share one processor core.","D":"the address of the next instruction"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C1B-05","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An assembler translates:","options":{"A":"assembly language into machine code","B":"SQL into HTML","C":"larger and slower than external storage","D":"Python into pseudocode"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Assembly instructions are converted into machine instructions.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: An assembler translates:","options":{"A":"assembly language into machine code","B":"a database table","C":"the physical length of a password","D":"the number of users with admin rights"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-06","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"The ALU performs:","options":{"A":"Store data permanently without a hard disk.","B":"arithmetic and logical operations","C":"network authentication","D":"screen rendering only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"The arithmetic logic unit is part of the processor datapath.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: The ALU performs:","options":{"A":"no connected devices","B":"arithmetic and logical operations","C":"only offline computers","D":"Write directly to the processor's registers at any time."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C1B-07","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Pipelining can improve performance by:","options":{"A":"Take permanent control of the processor from the OS.","B":"removing the need for registers","C":"overlapping stages of instruction execution","D":"storing all data on a network"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Pipelining increases throughput, although hazards can reduce the benefit.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: Pipelining can improve performance by:","options":{"A":"Stores data permanently when the power is switched off.","B":"Increases the total amount of addressable main memory.","C":"overlapping stages of instruction execution","D":"Allows several processes to share one processor core."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-08","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Cache memory is useful because it is:","options":{"A":"The instruction currently being decoded by the control unit.","B":"used only for backups","C":"a form of optical communication","D":"faster than main memory and stores frequently used data"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Caches exploit temporal and spatial locality.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Cache memory is useful because it is:","options":{"A":"Store data permanently without a hard disk.","B":"Write directly to the processor's registers at any time.","C":"Take permanent control of the processor from the OS.","D":"faster than main memory and stores frequently used data"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C1B-09","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Two's complement is used to represent:","options":{"A":"positive and negative binary integers","B":"Removes the need to store a sign bit at all.","C":"network domains","D":"SQL relationships"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Two's complement simplifies signed arithmetic in hardware.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: Two's complement is used to represent:","options":{"A":"positive and negative binary integers","B":"Functions with no side effects and immutable data.","C":"Rules and facts queried by an inference engine.","D":"Removing unnecessary detail to focus on the essentials."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-10","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A left binary shift by one place usually:","options":{"A":"divides by two","B":"multiplies an unsigned integer by two","C":"adds one","D":"Stores the magnitude and sign in two separate bytes."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Bits may be lost if the value exceeds the available width.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A left binary shift by one place usually:","options":{"A":"Runs concurrently with all the other stages.","B":"multiplies an unsigned integer by two","C":"Can be skipped if the deadline is tight.","D":"Is repeated in short cycles with user feedback."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-11","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An image's colour depth describes:","options":{"A":"Always produces a smaller file than lossy compression does.","B":"the number of images in a folder","C":"the number of bits used to represent each pixel's colour","D":"the speed of a printer"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Higher colour depth allows more possible colours per pixel.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: An image's colour depth describes:","options":{"A":"Shorter than 1000 items in total.","B":"Increase the number of bits used for each individual sample.","C":"the number of bits used to represent each pixel's colour","D":"Code completion, which suggests identifier names."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-12","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Sampling rate is the number of audio samples:","options":{"A":"stored per pixel","B":"sent per IP address","C":"used per database row","D":"taken per second"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A higher sampling rate can represent higher frequencies but increases data size.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: Sampling rate is the number of audio samples:","options":{"A":"Built entirely from unique prime numbers.","B":"Traversed using a post-order algorithm.","C":"Uses fewer characters than infix in every case.","D":"taken per second"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-13","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A lossy image format reduces file size by:","options":{"A":"discarding information judged less noticeable","B":"10000101, using a sign bit with the magnitude.","C":"using no compression","D":"adding extra pixels"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Lossy compression trades exact reconstruction for smaller files.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A lossy image format reduces file size by:","options":{"A":"discarding information judged less noticeable","B":"Translate assembly mnemonics into machine code.","C":"Check the source code for syntax errors.","D":"Allocate processor time between running processes."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-14","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A parity bit is used mainly to:","options":{"A":"11111010, by inverting the bits of 5 only.","B":"detect some single-bit errors","C":"increase bandwidth","D":"identify a website"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Parity adds a check bit but cannot correct every error.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A parity bit is used mainly to:","options":{"A":"A AND B","B":"detect some single-bit errors","C":"00000101, since the sign is stored separately.","D":"a network protocol"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-15","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Packet switching sends data as:","options":{"A":"Can only be used for video streaming applications.","B":"a single unbroken analogue wave","C":"small packets that may take different routes","D":"a database table"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Packets are reassembled at the destination.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: Packet switching sends data as:","options":{"A":"network authentication","B":"screen rendering only","C":"small packets that may take different routes","D":"The transport layer, which splits data into segments and checks delivery."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-16","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A network's bandwidth measures:","options":{"A":"the physical length of a password","B":"the number of users with admin rights","C":"the delay of one instruction","D":"the maximum data rate of a connection"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Bandwidth is capacity; latency is delay.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A network's bandwidth measures:","options":{"A":"divides by two","B":"adds one","C":"Layer 4 — Transport, using port numbers.","D":"the maximum data rate of a connection"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C1B-17","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Latency is:","options":{"A":"the delay before data transfer or response occurs","B":"Sends data without establishing a connection first.","C":"the storage capacity of RAM","D":"the strength of encryption"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Low latency matters for interactive applications.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: Latency is:","options":{"A":"the delay before data transfer or response occurs","B":"adding extra pixels","C":"Reserves a dedicated path for the whole conversation.","D":"increase bandwidth"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-18","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An IPv6 address is longer than IPv4 mainly because:","options":{"A":"it stores more user passwords","B":"it provides a much larger address space","C":"it removes routing","D":"Layer 2 — Data Link, using MAC addresses."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses compared with IPv4's 32-bit addresses.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: An IPv6 address is longer than IPv4 mainly because:","options":{"A":"the storage capacity of RAM","B":"it provides a much larger address space","C":"the strength of encryption","D":"Can only be used across a single local network."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-19","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A peer-to-peer network has:","options":{"A":"Requires all data to travel by an identical route.","B":"no connected devices","C":"nodes that can act as both clients and servers","D":"only offline computers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Peer-to-peer systems distribute roles but can be harder to manage centrally.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A peer-to-peer network has:","options":{"A":"a type of CPU cache","B":"Reversible, so the original password can be recovered.","C":"nodes that can act as both clients and servers","D":"a database backup only"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C1B-20","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A client-server system centralises:","options":{"A":"all keyboards in one device","B":"every user's personal password","C":"Compressible, so hashes take little storage.","D":"services or resources on dedicated servers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Central servers simplify management but can become a bottleneck or point of failure.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: A client-server system centralises:","options":{"A":"Requires frequent access to a single shared variable.","B":"Variable-length instructions that each do more work.","C":"Microcode to interpret every complex instruction.","D":"services or resources on dedicated servers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-3.2"},{"id":"CS-C1B-21","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An API is:","options":{"A":"a defined interface allowing software components to communicate","B":"a type of CPU cache","C":"Is faster because it sends data without waiting for acknowledgement.","D":"a database backup only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"APIs specify how one system can request services from another.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: An API is:","options":{"A":"a defined interface allowing software components to communicate","B":"True, for every possible value of A.","C":"The same value as A.","D":"TCP cannot handle the video file formats used in live streaming."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-01","stem":"During the fetch-execute cycle, the program counter holds:","options":{"A":"The data most recently read from main memory.","B":"The address of the next instruction to be fetched","C":"The result of the last arithmetic operation performed.","D":"The instruction currently being decoded by the control unit."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Registers in the cycle: the Program Counter holds the address of the next instruction; the Memory Address Register holds the address being accessed; the Memory Data Register holds the data or instruction fetched; the Current Instruction Register holds the instruction being decoded; the Accumulator holds arithmetic results. Fetch: PC to MAR, memory to MDR, MDR to CIR, PC incremented. Then decode and execute.","reforge":{"stem":"Which register holds the address of the memory location currently being accessed?","options":{"A":"The Current Instruction Register.","B":"The Program Counter.","C":"The Accumulator.","D":"The Memory Address Register"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-02","stem":"Cache memory improves performance mainly because it:","options":{"A":"Stores data permanently when the power is switched off.","B":"Increases the total amount of addressable main memory.","C":"Holds frequently used data closer and faster than RAM","D":"Allows several processes to share one processor core."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"The memory hierarchy trades speed against capacity and cost: registers, then L1/L2/L3 cache, then RAM, then secondary storage. Cache is small, fast, and holds recently or frequently used instructions and data, exploiting locality of reference. A cache hit avoids a slow RAM access; a miss costs extra time. More cache helps until the working set fits, after which the benefit falls away — which is why cache size alone does not determine speed.","reforge":{"stem":"Cache is effective largely because programs show locality of reference, meaning they:","options":{"A":"Tend to reuse the same data and nearby addresses","B":"Always execute instructions in a random order.","C":"Store all their variables in secondary storage.","D":"Use exactly the same amount of memory throughout."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-03","stem":"Increasing the number of processor cores gives the greatest benefit when the workload:","options":{"A":"Consists of one long strictly sequential calculation.","B":"Is limited by the speed of the hard disk.","C":"Requires frequent access to a single shared variable.","D":"Can be divided into tasks that run independently"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Extra cores help only where work can run in parallel. A strictly sequential task gains nothing, and Amdahl's law says overall speed-up is limited by the fraction that must stay sequential. Parallel work also brings overhead: splitting tasks, communication, and synchronising shared data. Other factors affecting performance are clock speed, cache size, word length, bus width, and whether the bottleneck is actually the processor at all.","reforge":{"stem":"A program runs no faster on a quad-core processor than on a single core. The most likely reason is that it:","options":{"A":"Uses too little RAM for the extra cores.","B":"Cannot be divided into parallel tasks","C":"Has an unusually large instruction set.","D":"Was written in a high-level language."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-04","stem":"A RISC processor differs from a CISC processor because RISC uses:","options":{"A":"A small set of simple, fixed-length instructions","B":"Variable-length instructions that each do more work.","C":"Microcode to interpret every complex instruction.","D":"Fewer registers but a much larger cache."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"RISC: few simple instructions, fixed length, mostly one cycle each, many registers, easy to pipeline, lower power — dominant in mobile devices. CISC: many complex variable-length instructions, each possibly several cycles, implemented in microcode, fewer registers, so compilers emit fewer instructions per task. RISC needs more instructions but executes them faster and pipelines them more readily, which is why power efficiency favours it.","reforge":{"stem":"Pipelining improves processor throughput by:","options":{"A":"Executing every instruction in a single clock cycle.","B":"Removing the need for the fetch-execute cycle.","C":"Overlapping the stages of consecutive instructions","D":"Storing instructions permanently in cache memory."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-05","stem":"Virtual memory allows a computer to:","options":{"A":"Increase the physical amount of RAM installed.","B":"Use secondary storage as an extension of RAM","C":"Run programs entirely inside the processor cache.","D":"Store data permanently without a hard disk."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"Virtual memory lets the operating system move pages of memory between RAM and a swap area on disk, so programs larger than physical RAM can run and each process sees its own address space. The cost is speed: disk is far slower than RAM. If too little RAM is available the system spends most of its time swapping pages rather than executing — thrashing — and the cure is more RAM or fewer concurrent processes.","reforge":{"stem":"A computer becomes extremely slow with constant disk activity when many programs are open. This is:","options":{"A":"A cache miss cascade in the processor.","B":"Fragmentation of the file allocation table.","C":"A scheduling deadlock between processes.","D":"Thrashing, from excessive page swapping"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-06","stem":"An interrupt allows a peripheral device to:","options":{"A":"Write directly to the processor's registers at any time.","B":"Take permanent control of the processor from the OS.","C":"Signal the processor that it needs attention","D":"Increase the clock speed while transferring data."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-OPERATING-SYSTEMS","scaffold":"An interrupt is a signal that something needs attention. At the end of the current fetch-execute cycle the processor checks the interrupt register; if a higher-priority interrupt is pending it pushes the current contents of its registers onto a stack, runs the interrupt service routine, then pops them back and resumes. This avoids polling, where the processor repeatedly checks devices and wastes cycles doing so.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are the contents of the registers pushed onto a stack before an interrupt is serviced?","options":{"A":"So the interrupted program can resume correctly","B":"So the interrupt can be given a higher priority.","C":"So the data can be written to secondary storage.","D":"So the processor can increase its clock speed."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-07","stem":"The Boolean expression A AND (NOT A) always evaluates to:","options":{"A":"The opposite value to A.","B":"True, for every possible value of A.","C":"The same value as A.","D":"False, for every possible value of A"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"A AND NOT A is always false, and A OR NOT A is always true — the complement laws. Other identities worth knowing: A AND A = A, A OR A = A (idempotence); A AND 1 = A, A OR 0 = A (identity); A AND 0 = 0, A OR 1 = 1 (annulment); and De Morgan's laws, NOT(A AND B) = NOT A OR NOT B, NOT(A OR B) = NOT A AND NOT B. These simplify circuits and reduce the number of logic gates required.","reforge":{"stem":"Applying De Morgan's law, NOT (A OR B) is equivalent to:","options":{"A":"NOT A OR NOT B.","B":"NOT A AND NOT B","C":"A OR NOT B.","D":"A AND B."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-08","stem":"A half adder differs from a full adder because a half adder:","options":{"A":"Cannot accept a carry input","B":"Produces a sum but never a carry output.","C":"Uses only OR gates in its construction.","D":"Adds two complete bytes rather than two bits."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"A half adder adds two bits, producing sum (XOR) and carry (AND), but has no carry input, so it can only be used for the least significant bit. A full adder takes three inputs — two bits plus a carry in — and produces sum and carry out, so full adders can be chained into a ripple carry adder for multi-bit addition. Understanding this explains how the ALU performs arithmetic from simple gates.","reforge":{"stem":"In a half adder, the sum output is produced by which gate?","options":{"A":"An AND gate, which also gives the carry.","B":"An OR gate combining both inputs.","C":"An XOR gate, 1 when inputs differ","D":"A NOT gate applied to one input."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-09","stem":"A procedural program differs from an object-oriented one because a procedural program is organised around:","options":{"A":"Classes that bundle attributes with their methods.","B":"Sequences of instructions grouped into procedures","C":"Functions with no side effects and immutable data.","D":"Rules and facts queried by an inference engine."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"Procedural: instructions grouped into procedures acting on separate data structures. Object-oriented: state and behaviour bundled into objects, supporting encapsulation, inheritance and polymorphism, which suits large systems needing reuse. Functional: computation as evaluation of functions without side effects, with immutable data, which makes reasoning and parallelism easier. Declarative and logic paradigms state what is required and let the system determine how.","reforge":{"stem":"A key characteristic of the functional paradigm is that functions:","options":{"A":"Must always be defined inside a class.","B":"Always modify the variables passed to them.","C":"Can only be called from the main program.","D":"Produce no side effects"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-10","stem":"Decomposition as a computational thinking technique means:","options":{"A":"Removing unnecessary detail to focus on the essentials.","B":"Identifying patterns shared between different problems.","C":"Breaking a problem into smaller manageable sub-problems","D":"Writing a step-by-step sequence to solve a problem."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"Computational thinking: decomposition breaks a problem into smaller parts that can be solved and tested separately; abstraction removes irrelevant detail so the essential structure is visible; pattern recognition spots similarities that let existing solutions be reused; algorithmic thinking sets out the ordered steps. They are distinct — a question describing 'ignoring detail that does not matter' is abstraction, not decomposition.","reforge":{"stem":"A map of the underground railway omits actual distances and street layout. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Abstraction, because irrelevant detail is removed","B":"Decomposition, because the network is split up.","C":"Pattern recognition, because lines look similar.","D":"Algorithmic thinking, because routes are ordered."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-N3-11","stem":"Which situation shows a stack overflow rather than a logic error?","options":{"A":"A comparison using > where >= was required.","B":"A loop that runs one more time than intended.","C":"An average calculated by dividing by the wrong total.","D":"A recursive function without a reachable base case"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Each function call pushes a stack frame holding parameters, local variables and the return address. Unbounded recursion keeps pushing frames until the stack's memory is exhausted, producing a stack overflow — a runtime error that halts the program. The other options describe logic errors: the program runs to completion but produces the wrong answer, and only testing against expected results reveals them.","reforge":{"stem":"Boundary testing of a function accepting marks from 0 to 100 should include:","options":{"A":"Only typical values such as 45 and 72.","B":"Values at and either side of 0 and 100","C":"Random values chosen automatically at runtime.","D":"Only values far outside the range, such as 5000."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N3-12","stem":"White box testing differs from black box testing because white box testing:","options":{"A":"Examines the internal code and its execution paths","B":"Is always performed by the end users of the system.","C":"Tests only the inputs and outputs of the program.","D":"Can only be carried out after the system is released."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"White box testing uses knowledge of the code to exercise particular paths, branches and conditions, aiming for coverage. Black box testing ignores the internals and checks that given inputs produce expected outputs, including normal, boundary and erroneous data. Alpha testing happens in-house, beta testing with real users before release. A test plan should record the data used, the reason for choosing it, and the expected result.","reforge":{"stem":"Erroneous test data for a field expecting a positive integer would be:","options":{"A":"The value 1, the smallest valid entry.","B":"The value 50, a typical valid entry.","C":"The text 'seven', not an integer","D":"The value 100, the largest valid entry."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-13","stem":"In the waterfall model of development, each stage:","options":{"A":"Runs concurrently with all the other stages.","B":"Is completed before the next one begins","C":"Can be skipped if the deadline is tight.","D":"Is repeated in short cycles with user feedback."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"Waterfall runs analysis, design, implementation, testing, maintenance in sequence — clear documentation and milestones, but requirements are fixed early and change is expensive, so it suits stable, safety-critical projects. Agile works in short iterations delivering working software, with continuous user involvement, welcoming changing requirements but producing less documentation and less predictable overall cost. The choice depends on how stable the requirements are.","reforge":{"stem":"Agile development is usually preferred when:","options":{"A":"Every requirement is known precisely at the start.","B":"No user contact is possible during development.","C":"Formal documentation is the main deliverable.","D":"Requirements are likely to change"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-14","stem":"Big O notation describes an algorithm's:","options":{"A":"Exact running time in seconds on given hardware.","B":"Total number of lines of source code.","C":"How resource use grows as input size increases","D":"Number of syntax errors found during compilation."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Big O expresses how time or space requirements scale with input size n, ignoring constants and hardware. Common orders from best: O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic (binary search), O(n) linear (linear search), O(n log n) (merge and quick sort), O(n²) quadratic (bubble and insertion sort), O(2ⁿ) exponential. For small n a 'worse' algorithm may be faster in practice; Big O matters as n grows large.","reforge":{"stem":"Which time complexity describes an algorithm whose running time is unaffected by input size?","options":{"A":"O(1), constant time","B":"O(n), linear time.","C":"O(log n), logarithmic time.","D":"O(n²), quadratic time."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"},{"id":"CS-N3-15","stem":"Merge sort is described as a divide-and-conquer algorithm because it:","options":{"A":"Compares each adjacent pair and swaps them repeatedly.","B":"Inserts each item into its correct position in turn.","C":"Selects the smallest remaining item on every pass.","D":"Splits the list, sorts each half, then merges them"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Merge sort recursively halves the list until single elements remain, then merges sorted sublists back together — O(n log n) in all cases, stable, but needing extra memory proportional to n. Quick sort partitions around a pivot: O(n log n) on average and in place, but O(n²) if pivots are chosen badly. Bubble, insertion and selection sorts are O(n²) but simple, and insertion sort performs well on nearly sorted data.","reforge":{"stem":"Quick sort degrades to O(n²) when the chosen pivot:","options":{"A":"Is always the median value of the list.","B":"Is repeatedly the smallest or largest","C":"Is selected at random each time.","D":"Divides the list into two equal halves."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N3-16","stem":"A binary search requires that the list being searched is:","options":{"A":"Sorted into order before the search begins","B":"Made up entirely of unique integer values.","C":"Stored in a linked list rather than an array.","D":"Shorter than 1000 items in total."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Binary search compares the target with the middle element and discards the half that cannot contain it, repeating until found or the range is empty — O(log n), but only valid on sorted data. Linear search works on unsorted data at O(n). If a list is searched once, sorting first (at least O(n log n)) costs more than a single linear search; if it is searched repeatedly, sorting once and using binary search pays off.","reforge":{"stem":"A list will be searched thousands of times. Sorting it once first is worthwhile because:","options":{"A":"Sorting reduces the memory the list occupies.","B":"Linear search cannot be used on large lists.","C":"Repeated binary searches beat linear ones","D":"Sorting removes any duplicate entries automatically."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N3-17","stem":"Which feature of an integrated development environment helps most when locating a logic error?","options":{"A":"Syntax highlighting, which colours language keywords.","B":"A debugger with breakpoints and variable watches","C":"Code completion, which suggests identifier names.","D":"Auto-indentation, which formats the code layout."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"An IDE bundles an editor, translator and debugging tools. Syntax highlighting, auto-indentation and code completion speed up writing and help avoid syntax errors, but none of them reveal a logic error, because the code is valid. A debugger does: breakpoints pause execution at a chosen line, stepping runs one statement at a time, and variable watches show values changing, so the point where actual behaviour diverges from intended behaviour can be found.","reforge":{"stem":"Stepping through code one line at a time while watching variable values is called:","options":{"A":"Compiling, which translates the whole program at once.","B":"Profiling, which measures memory usage.","C":"Linking, which combines object files.","D":"Tracing, which follows execution step by step"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-18","stem":"In an immediate addressing instruction, the operand holds:","options":{"A":"The address of a register containing an address.","B":"The memory address holding the value.","C":"The value to be used directly","D":"An offset added to the program counter."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Addressing modes: immediate — the operand is the value itself, fast but limited in size; direct — the operand is a memory address holding the value; indirect — the operand gives an address that holds the address of the value, allowing pointers; indexed — a base address plus the contents of an index register, which is how arrays are accessed efficiently. Each trades flexibility against the number of memory accesses required.","reforge":{"stem":"Indexed addressing is particularly useful for:","options":{"A":"Stepping through the elements of an array","B":"Storing a single constant value in a register.","C":"Preventing the program counter from incrementing.","D":"Translating assembly into machine code."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N3-19","stem":"A queue implemented as a linked list rather than a fixed array mainly gains:","options":{"A":"Automatic sorting of the items it contains.","B":"Faster access to an element by its index position.","C":"Guaranteed contiguous storage in memory.","D":"The ability to grow without a fixed capacity"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"An array-based queue has a fixed capacity, needs a circular implementation to reuse freed space, and offers direct indexed access. A linked-list queue grows and shrinks dynamically, with enqueue and dequeue as simple pointer changes, but uses extra memory for pointers, loses contiguous storage and cannot be indexed directly. The right choice depends on whether the maximum size is known and whether indexed access is needed.","reforge":{"stem":"A disadvantage of a linked-list implementation compared with an array is that:","options":{"A":"It cannot store items of different sizes.","B":"Finding the nth item means traversing the list","C":"Its maximum capacity is fixed at compile time.","D":"Insertion always requires shifting every element."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N3-20","stem":"A binary search tree offers O(log n) search only when the tree is:","options":{"A":"Reasonably balanced rather than skewed","B":"Stored in a contiguous block of memory.","C":"Built entirely from unique prime numbers.","D":"Traversed using a post-order algorithm."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"In a binary search tree, values smaller than a node go left and larger go right, so each comparison halves the remaining search space — O(log n) when the tree is balanced. Inserting already-sorted data produces a skewed tree that is effectively a linked list, giving O(n). Self-balancing variants such as AVL and red-black trees rotate nodes on insertion to keep the height logarithmic and guarantee the better bound.","reforge":{"stem":"Inserting the already-sorted values 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 into an empty binary search tree produces:","options":{"A":"A perfectly balanced tree of minimum height.","B":"A tree with two children at every level.","C":"A skewed tree behaving like a linked list","D":"An empty tree, since duplicates are rejected."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N3-21","stem":"Reverse Polish notation is used in expression evaluation because it:","options":{"A":"Uses fewer characters than infix in every case.","B":"Needs no brackets and suits stack evaluation","C":"Converts all operands into binary automatically.","D":"Can represent only addition and subtraction."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-STRUCTURES","scaffold":"In Reverse Polish (postfix) notation operators follow their operands, so precedence is unambiguous and brackets are unnecessary. Evaluation uses a stack: push operands, and on meeting an operator pop the required number, apply it, and push the result. Infix 3 + 4 × 2 becomes 3 4 2 × +. It corresponds to a post-order traversal of the expression tree, which is why compilers use it internally.","reforge":{"stem":"The infix expression (5 + 2) * 3 written in Reverse Polish notation is:","options":{"A":"5 2 3 + *","B":"5 + 2 * 3","C":"* + 5 2 3","D":"5 2 + 3 *"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N3-22","stem":"A compiler's lexical analysis stage is responsible for:","options":{"A":"Allocating memory addresses to each variable.","B":"Checking that the program's logic produces correct output.","C":"Grouping characters into tokens and removing whitespace","D":"Optimising the generated machine code for speed."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"Compilation stages: lexical analysis turns the character stream into tokens, strips whitespace and comments, and builds the symbol table; syntax analysis checks the tokens against the language grammar and builds a parse tree, reporting syntax errors; semantic analysis checks type compatibility and declarations; code generation produces object code; optimisation improves speed or size. No stage can detect a logic error — the program's intent is not knowable to the compiler.","reforge":{"stem":"Which error would a compiler be unable to detect?","options":{"A":"A mean calculated with the wrong divisor","B":"A missing closing bracket at the end of a long expression.","C":"Assigning a string value to an integer variable.","D":"A misspelled reserved keyword."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N5-07","stem":"Insertion sort performs particularly well when the input list is:","options":{"A":"In reverse order, its worst possible case.","B":"Made up entirely of identical values.","C":"Longer than several million items.","D":"Already nearly sorted"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ALGORITHMS","scaffold":"Insertion sort takes each item and moves it back past larger items into place. On nearly sorted data few shifts are needed, approaching O(n); on reverse-sorted data every item moves the whole way, giving O(n²). It is stable, sorts in place, and has low overhead, so library sorts often use it for small sublists inside a faster O(n log n) algorithm rather than recursing all the way down.","reforge":{"stem":"A sorting algorithm is described as stable when it:","options":{"A":"Never requires more memory than the list itself.","B":"Preserves the original order of equal-valued items","C":"Always completes in the same time regardless of input.","D":"Can be interrupted and resumed without error."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N5-08","stem":"A linker is needed after compilation in order to:","options":{"A":"Combine object files and libraries","B":"Translate assembly mnemonics into machine code.","C":"Check the source code for syntax errors.","D":"Allocate processor time between running processes."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-PROGRAMMING-ERRORS","scaffold":"A compiler turns each source file into an object file with unresolved references to code defined elsewhere. The linker resolves those references, combining object files with library routines into a single executable. Static linking copies library code into the executable, giving a larger but self-contained file; dynamic linking resolves at load time from shared libraries, keeping files smaller and allowing the library to be patched independently. A loader then places it in memory.","reforge":{"stem":"Dynamic linking rather than static linking keeps executables smaller because library code is:","options":{"A":"Compressed inside the executable file.","B":"Rewritten into a more compact instruction set.","C":"Shared at run time rather than copied in","D":"Discarded once the program has started."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.2"},{"id":"CS-N5-09","stem":"Which problem is undecidable, meaning no algorithm can solve it for every possible input?","options":{"A":"Sorting a list of integers into ascending order.","B":"Determining whether an arbitrary program will halt","C":"Finding the shortest path through a weighted graph.","D":"Testing whether a given number is prime."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ABSTRACTION","scaffold":"The halting problem is undecidable: Turing proved no general algorithm can determine, for every program and input, whether it terminates. This is a limit of computation itself, not of current hardware or cleverness. Distinguish it from intractable problems, which are solvable in principle but take impractically long as input grows, such as the travelling salesman problem by brute force — those become feasible again with heuristics giving good rather than optimal answers.","reforge":{"stem":"A problem described as intractable differs from an undecidable one because it:","options":{"A":"Has no algorithmic solution of any kind.","B":"Applies only to programs written recursively.","C":"Produces a different answer on each run.","D":"Can be solved, but not in a practical time"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-1.3"}]},"CS-4":{"label":"Component 2 — Architecture, Data, Communication & Applications","color":"#0f766e","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","questions":[{"id":"CS-C2B-01","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A Von Neumann architecture stores:","options":{"A":"instructions and data in the same memory","B":"instructions only on paper","C":"data only in the CPU cache","D":"The data most recently read from main memory."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"The shared memory model creates the Von Neumann bottleneck.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A Von Neumann architecture stores:","options":{"A":"instructions and data in the same memory","B":"The result of the last arithmetic operation performed.","C":"clearing all registers","D":"sending a packet to DNS"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-02","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"The fetch-decode-execute cycle begins by:","options":{"A":"The body always executes at least once regardless of the condition.","B":"fetching the instruction from the address in the program counter","C":"clearing all registers","D":"sending a packet to DNS"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"The instruction is fetched before it is decoded and executed.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: The fetch-decode-execute cycle begins by:","options":{"A":"a network with no software","B":"fetching the instruction from the address in the program counter","C":"A compiler executes code directly without producing any output file.","D":"increase RAM capacity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-C2B-03","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A GPU is especially suited to:","options":{"A":"Consists of one long strictly sequential calculation.","B":"storing relational keys","C":"many parallel operations such as graphics processing","D":"managing user permissions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"GPUs contain many processing elements designed for parallel workloads.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A GPU is especially suited to:","options":{"A":"network packets only","B":"Requires frequent access to a single shared variable.","C":"many parallel operations such as graphics processing","D":"finish all tasks immediately"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-04","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An embedded system is:","options":{"A":"HTTPS only uses symmetric encryption — asymmetric is for email.","B":"a database without a processor","C":"a network with no software","D":"a computer built into a larger device for a specific function"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Embedded systems often have limited resources and real-time requirements.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: An embedded system is:","options":{"A":"It converts the program into platform-independent machine code.","B":"encrypt a query","C":"change binary to decimal","D":"a computer built into a larger device for a specific function"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-05","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An interrupt allows a device to:","options":{"A":"signal the processor that it needs attention","B":"rewrite the operating system without permission","C":"increase RAM capacity","D":"translate a domain name"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Interrupts let the processor respond to events without constant polling.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: An interrupt allows a device to:","options":{"A":"signal the processor that it needs attention","B":"two identical secret keys","C":"Always execute instructions in a random order.","D":"a public IP address"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-06","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A real-time system must:","options":{"A":"Store all their variables in secondary storage.","B":"respond within defined timing constraints","C":"store every event permanently","D":"avoid all interrupts"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Correctness depends on both the result and when it is produced.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: A real-time system must:","options":{"A":"improving bandwidth","B":"respond within defined timing constraints","C":"checking a checksum","D":"Use exactly the same amount of memory throughout."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-07","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"The purpose of an operating-system scheduler is to:","options":{"A":"Is limited by the speed of the hard disk.","B":"compress audio samples","C":"allocate processor time among processes","D":"assign domain names"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Scheduling balances responsiveness, fairness and throughput.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: The purpose of an operating-system scheduler is to:","options":{"A":"Guarantees that all values become exact.","B":"Increases both the range and the precision.","C":"allocate processor time among processes","D":"Has no effect unless the sign bit also changes."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-08","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Paging divides memory into:","options":{"A":"variable-size database tables","B":"network packets only","C":"CPU instructions and passwords","D":"fixed-size pages and frames"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Paging supports virtual memory and reduces external fragmentation.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Paging divides memory into:","options":{"A":"Width divided by height, times colour depth.","B":"A single bit has been changed during transmission.","C":"The parity bit itself is corrupted in transit.","D":"fixed-size pages and frames"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-09","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A deadlock can occur when processes:","options":{"A":"wait indefinitely for resources held by one another","B":"Variable-length instructions that each do more work.","C":"share no resources","D":"use only read-only files"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Deadlock involves circular waiting and requires prevention or recovery.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A deadlock can occur when processes:","options":{"A":"wait indefinitely for resources held by one another","B":"Shared with any organisation that requests it.","C":"Executing every instruction in a single clock cycle.","D":"Only when the intruder causes damage to data."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-10","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A relational database stores data in:","options":{"A":"only a single unstructured paragraph","B":"tables made of rows and columns","C":"CPU registers","D":"network routers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Relations use records and attributes, with keys linking tables.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A relational database stores data in:","options":{"A":"Complete independence from any network connection.","B":"tables made of rows and columns","C":"A one-off cost with no ongoing charges.","D":"Full physical control over where data is stored."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-11","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A foreign key is used to:","options":{"A":"Slow query performance caused by joining too many tables simultaneously.","B":"encrypt a database field","C":"link a record to a primary key in another table","D":"sort every table automatically"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Foreign keys represent relationships between entities.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A foreign key is used to:","options":{"A":"A more secure form of encrypted data.","B":"A single shared backbone cable running past each node.","C":"link a record to a primary key in another table","D":"Every other device by a dedicated individual cable."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-12","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"An SQL JOIN is used to:","options":{"A":"Convert the database from SQL to NoSQL format.","B":"encrypt a query","C":"change binary to decimal","D":"combine related rows from tables"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Joins use related fields to bring data together.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: An SQL JOIN is used to:","options":{"A":"Transmit at a much lower overall data rate.","B":"Are always connected to a central switch.","C":"A physical cable standard for connecting devices.","D":"combine related rows from tables"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-13","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A distributed database stores data:","options":{"A":"across multiple networked locations or nodes","B":"only in one local variable","C":"inside a CPU register","D":"It enables priority-based removal of elements."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATABASES","scaffold":"Distribution can improve availability but adds consistency and coordination issues.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A distributed database stores data:","options":{"A":"across multiple networked locations or nodes","B":"A field that uniquely identifies each record in its own table.","C":"A larger instruction set for general tasks.","D":"A much higher clock speed on a single core."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-14","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Symmetric encryption uses:","options":{"A":"a public key only","B":"the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt","C":"no key at all","D":"Symmetric encryption cannot handle web traffic."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Symmetric encryption is fast but requires secure key sharing.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: Symmetric encryption uses:","options":{"A":"sending a packet to DNS","B":"the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt","C":"A trusted third party holds the key and decrypts messages on both sides.","D":"storing relational keys"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-15","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"Asymmetric encryption uses:","options":{"A":"one password shared publicly","B":"only a parity bit","C":"a public/private key pair","D":"two identical secret keys"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"A public key can be shared while the private key is kept secret.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: Asymmetric encryption uses:","options":{"A":"store every event permanently","B":"avoid all interrupts","C":"a public/private key pair","D":"convert source code into HTML"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-C2B-16","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A cryptographic hash is designed to be:","options":{"A":"Encryption is only used by governments.","B":"a public IP address","C":"a screen-resolution setting","D":"one-way and difficult to reverse"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Hashes support password storage and integrity checks, not decryption.","reforge":{"stem":"In a systems-design scenario, consider this question: A cryptographic hash is designed to be:","options":{"A":"only a single unstructured paragraph","B":"CPU registers","C":"network routers","D":"one-way and difficult to reverse"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-C2B-17","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A digital certificate helps verify:","options":{"A":"that a public key is associated with a stated identity","B":"that a file has no copyright","C":"that a CPU is overheating","D":"Symmetric encryption cannot physically be used for internet-based communication."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"Certificate authorities bind identities to public keys.","reforge":{"stem":"A technician applies the idea while troubleshooting: A digital certificate helps verify:","options":{"A":"that a public key is associated with a stated identity","B":"Public keys are longer and therefore provide stronger encryption by default.","C":"a public key only","D":"no key at all"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-18","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"HTTPS protects web communication mainly through:","options":{"A":"To remove all responsibility for complying with licence terms","B":"TLS encryption and authenticated connections","C":"removing all cookies","D":"using only plain HTTP"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-CRYPTOGRAPHY","scaffold":"TLS provides confidentiality and integrity in transit, but not protection from every scam.","reforge":{"stem":"A software team encounters the following situation: HTTPS protects web communication mainly through:","options":{"A":"that a CPU is overheating","B":"TLS encryption and authenticated connections","C":"that a database has no null values","D":"The ciphertext is always longer than the plaintext."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-19","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A denial-of-service attack affects availability by:","options":{"A":"UDP is more reliable than TCP for large file transfers where accuracy matters most.","B":"improving bandwidth","C":"making a service too busy to respond to legitimate requests","D":"checking a checksum"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Availability is one part of the confidentiality, integrity and availability model.","reforge":{"stem":"A computing case presents the following problem: A denial-of-service attack affects availability by:","options":{"A":"Asymmetric encryption is mathematically unbreakable; symmetric encryption is not.","B":"replaces access control","C":"making a service too busy to respond to legitimate requests","D":"makes a network faster"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-20","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"The Data Protection Act and UK GDPR are concerned with:","options":{"A":"Removing viruses already installed on a workstation.","B":"the design of CPU registers","C":"the colour of web pages","D":"how personal data is collected, used and protected"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Organisations need lawful, fair and secure handling of personal data.","reforge":{"stem":"A developer tests this principle with a new example: The Data Protection Act and UK GDPR are concerned with:","options":{"A":"A larger range for the same number of bits.","B":"Creating backups of data before it leaves the network.","C":"The colour depth is automatically reduced.","D":"how personal data is collected, used and protected"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-C2B-21","spec":"EDUQAS A LEVEL COMPUTER SCIENCE","stem":"A backup is valuable because it:","options":{"A":"allows data to be restored after loss or corruption","B":"Every differential taken since the last full backup.","C":"replaces access control","D":"makes a network faster"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-SECURITY","scaffold":"Backups support recovery, especially when stored separately from the original data.","reforge":{"stem":"During a practical programming task, a student asks: A backup is valuable because it:","options":{"A":"allows data to be restored after loss or corruption","B":"Stores data on rotating magnetised platters.","C":"Priority-based — items are removed in order of their assigned priority.","D":"Compressing data so more fits on each disk."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.1"},{"id":"CS-N4-01","stem":"Adding the 8-bit two's complement numbers 01010000 and 01100000 produces an overflow because the result:","options":{"A":"Contains more than four set bits in total.","B":"Exceeds the range representable in 8 bits","C":"Requires a carry out of the least significant bit.","D":"Cannot be converted back into denary."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"In 8-bit two's complement the range is −128 to +127. 01010000 is 80 and 01100000 is 96; their sum, 176, exceeds 127. The tell-tale sign is that adding two positives gives a result with the sign bit set — here 10110000, which reads as −80. Overflow is detected when the carry into the sign bit differs from the carry out of it. Adding numbers of opposite sign can never overflow.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding two negative 8-bit two's complement numbers gives a result with a 0 in the sign bit. This indicates:","options":{"A":"A rounding error in the fractional part.","B":"Normal behaviour, since negatives sum to a positive.","C":"An underflow of the mantissa.","D":"Overflow, as two negatives cannot sum positive"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-02","stem":"In a floating point number, increasing the number of bits given to the exponent rather than the mantissa:","options":{"A":"Guarantees that all values become exact.","B":"Increases both the range and the precision.","C":"Increases the range but reduces the precision","D":"Has no effect unless the sign bit also changes."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A floating point number splits its bits between mantissa and exponent. The mantissa sets precision — how many significant figures can be held; the exponent sets range — how large or small a magnitude is possible. With a fixed total width the two trade off directly. Normalisation maximises precision by ensuring the mantissa starts 0.1 for positives or 1.0 for negatives, so no bits are wasted on leading duplicates of the sign bit.","reforge":{"stem":"Normalising a floating point mantissa is done in order to:","options":{"A":"Use the mantissa bits for maximum precision","B":"Convert the number into two's complement form.","C":"Guarantee the number can be stored without overflow.","D":"Reduce the number of bits the exponent requires."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-03","stem":"A fixed point representation differs from floating point because fixed point has:","options":{"A":"No ability to represent negative values at all.","B":"An exponent field that scales the value stored.","C":"A larger range for the same number of bits.","D":"A binary point in a set position"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Fixed point places the binary point at a set position, so the absolute precision is constant and arithmetic is fast and predictable — useful in financial and embedded systems. Floating point moves the point using an exponent, giving a far greater range for the same width, but the spacing between representable values grows with magnitude and most decimal fractions cannot be stored exactly. That inexactness is why equality comparisons on floats are unreliable.","reforge":{"stem":"Comparing two floating point values with = is unreliable mainly because:","options":{"A":"Floating point values are stored as text internally.","B":"Many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly","C":"The exponent is always rounded up to the nearest power.","D":"Floating point numbers cannot hold negative values."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-04","stem":"A bitmap image scaled up to twice its original size typically appears blurred because:","options":{"A":"A fixed pixel count is stretched over more area","B":"The file is recompressed using a lossy algorithm.","C":"The colour depth is automatically reduced.","D":"The image is converted into a vector representation."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A bitmap stores a grid of pixels, so enlarging it stretches a fixed pixel count over more space and the software must interpolate, producing blur or visible blocks. A vector image stores shapes as mathematical descriptions — points, lines, curves and fills — so it can be scaled to any size without loss and usually needs less storage for flat-colour graphics. Photographs suit bitmaps; logos and diagrams suit vectors.","reforge":{"stem":"Which image would be most suitable for storing in a vector format?","options":{"A":"A photograph of a landscape with fine detail.","B":"A scanned page with handwritten annotations.","C":"A company logo made of flat shapes and text","D":"A textured background with random noise."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-05","stem":"The file size of an uncompressed bitmap is calculated as:","options":{"A":"Width plus height plus colour depth.","B":"Width times height times colour depth","C":"Colour depth divided by the number of pixels.","D":"Width divided by height, times colour depth."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"File size in bits = width in pixels × height in pixels × colour depth in bits; divide by 8 for bytes, by 1024 for kibibytes. Colour depth of n bits allows 2ⁿ colours, so 8 bits gives 256 and 24 bits gives about 16.7 million. Doubling both width and height quadruples the pixel count and so the size. Metadata adds a small overhead beyond the pixel data itself.","reforge":{"stem":"An image is 100 × 200 pixels with a colour depth of 8 bits. Its uncompressed size is:","options":{"A":"1,600 bytes, using 8 bits per row.","B":"160,000 bytes, multiplying all three values.","C":"2,500 bytes, dividing the pixel count by 8.","D":"20,000 bytes, one byte per pixel"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-06","stem":"Parity checking can fail to detect an error when:","options":{"A":"A single bit has been changed during transmission.","B":"The parity bit itself is corrupted in transit.","C":"An even number of bits has been changed","D":"The data is transmitted at a very high speed."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"A parity bit makes the number of 1s odd or even as agreed. A single flipped bit changes the count and is detected; two flipped bits restore the original parity and pass undetected. Parity detects but cannot correct. Checksums add values across a block and detect more errors; cyclic redundancy checks use polynomial division and are stronger still; majority voting and Hamming codes can locate and correct errors at the cost of extra bits.","reforge":{"stem":"Which technique can not only detect an error but also correct it?","options":{"A":"A Hamming code using parity bits","B":"A checksum appended to the data block.","C":"A cyclic redundancy check on the frame.","D":"A single parity bit added to each byte."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-07","stem":"Solid state storage differs from a magnetic hard disk because solid state storage:","options":{"A":"Offers unlimited write cycles without degradation.","B":"Stores data on rotating magnetised platters.","C":"Requires constant power to retain its contents.","D":"Uses flash memory with no moving parts"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"Solid state drives use NAND flash: no moving parts, so they are fast, silent, shock-resistant and low-power, but cost more per gigabyte and have a finite number of write cycles per cell. Magnetic disks use rotating platters and moving heads: cheaper per gigabyte and long-lived for archives, but slower, noisier and fragile. Optical media are cheap and portable but slow and low-capacity. All three are non-volatile, unlike RAM.","reforge":{"stem":"Which storage type is volatile, losing its contents when power is removed?","options":{"A":"A solid state drive using flash memory.","B":"RAM used as the computer's main memory","C":"An optical disc such as a DVD.","D":"A magnetic hard disk drive."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-08","stem":"RAID 1 improves on a single disk mainly by:","options":{"A":"Mirroring data so a disk failure loses nothing","B":"Compressing data so more fits on each disk.","C":"Splitting data across disks for greater speed.","D":"Encrypting data automatically as it is written."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"RAID 0 stripes data across disks for speed and full capacity but no redundancy — one failure loses everything. RAID 1 mirrors identical copies, so a failure loses nothing, at the cost of half the usable capacity. RAID 5 stripes with distributed parity, surviving one disk failure with better capacity efficiency. Note RAID protects against hardware failure, not against deletion, ransomware or fire, so it is not a substitute for backups.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a RAID array not a substitute for taking backups?","options":{"A":"RAID arrays are slower than a single disk.","B":"RAID cannot be used with solid state drives.","C":"It does not protect against deletion","D":"RAID stores only compressed copies of files."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-09","stem":"Under UK data protection law, personal data must be:","options":{"A":"Retained permanently in case it is needed later.","B":"Kept no longer than necessary for its purpose","C":"Shared with any organisation that requests it.","D":"Stored only on servers outside the United Kingdom."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Data protection principles: process lawfully, fairly and transparently; collect for specified purposes only; collect only what is adequate and relevant (data minimisation); keep it accurate and up to date; retain no longer than necessary; keep it secure; and be accountable. Individuals have rights of access, rectification, erasure, portability and objection. Breaches affecting rights must be reported to the regulator, normally within 72 hours.","reforge":{"stem":"A website collects a date of birth it has no use for. Which principle does this breach?","options":{"A":"Integrity, since the data is not encrypted.","B":"Accuracy, since the date may be entered wrongly.","C":"Storage limitation, since data is kept too long.","D":"Data minimisation: collect only what is needed"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-10","stem":"Under the Computer Misuse Act, gaining access to a system without permission is an offence:","options":{"A":"Only when the intruder causes damage to data.","B":"Only where the system belongs to a government body.","C":"Even if nothing is altered or copied","D":"Only if the intruder profits financially from it."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"The Computer Misuse Act creates offences of unauthorised access (the basic offence, committed even if nothing is changed), unauthorised access with intent to commit a further offence, unauthorised modification of material, and making or supplying tools for such purposes. Authorisation is the deciding factor, which is why penetration testers require explicit written permission before testing a client's systems.","reforge":{"stem":"A penetration tester avoids committing an offence under the Act because they have:","options":{"A":"Written authorisation from the owner","B":"Used only tools that are freely available.","C":"Avoided modifying any of the data found.","D":"Published their findings openly afterwards."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.5"},{"id":"CS-N4-11","stem":"Open source software differs from proprietary software because open source:","options":{"A":"May never be used for commercial purposes.","B":"Is always free of any charge to the end user.","C":"Comes with a guaranteed support contract.","D":"Makes the source code available to modify"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Open source licences grant the right to read, modify and redistribute the source, often requiring derivative works to carry the same licence. Advantages: adaptable, auditable, no licence fee, community fixes. Disadvantages: support may be informal, and expertise is needed to adapt it. Proprietary software keeps source closed, provides formal support and warranties, but ties the user to the vendor. Note open source is about rights, not price.","reforge":{"stem":"A likely disadvantage of adopting open source software in a business is:","options":{"A":"The source code cannot be inspected for flaws.","B":"Formal vendor support may not be available","C":"The software can never be modified in-house.","D":"Licence fees increase with each additional user."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-12","stem":"An algorithm used to decide loan applications is found to reject one demographic group far more often. The most likely explanation is that:","options":{"A":"Bias in the historical training data has been learned","B":"The algorithm was written in a low-level language.","C":"Machine learning models cannot process numerical data.","D":"The training set was too small to run at all."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"A model learns the patterns in its training data, including historical human bias, and can reproduce or amplify it while appearing objective. Mitigations: audit training data for representativeness, test outcomes across groups, keep humans in the loop for consequential decisions, and require explainability so a decision can be challenged. Accountability is a live issue since responsibility may sit with the developer, deployer or data provider.","reforge":{"stem":"Requiring an automated decision system to be explainable matters mainly because it:","options":{"A":"Guarantees the system will never make an error.","B":"Reduces the processing power the model needs.","C":"Allows people to understand and challenge it","D":"Removes the need to test the system before release."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N4-13","stem":"Compared with an on-premises server, cloud computing typically offers:","options":{"A":"Complete independence from any network connection.","B":"Capacity that can be scaled up or down on demand","C":"A one-off cost with no ongoing charges.","D":"Full physical control over where data is stored."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-ETHICS","scaffold":"Cloud computing offers elastic capacity, access from anywhere, and shifts maintenance and hardware cost to the provider, replacing capital expenditure with ongoing subscription. Drawbacks: dependence on connectivity, ongoing cost that can exceed ownership over time, reduced control over data location which raises legal and compliance questions, and the risk of vendor lock-in when migrating away proves expensive.","reforge":{"stem":"A significant legal concern when storing personal data with a cloud provider is:","options":{"A":"Cloud providers never offer service guarantees.","B":"Cloud storage cannot be encrypted at rest.","C":"Data in the cloud cannot be backed up.","D":"The servers may be in another jurisdiction"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-14","stem":"An embedded system differs from a general-purpose computer because it is:","options":{"A":"Always more powerful than a desktop machine.","B":"Unable to accept any input from sensors.","C":"Designed for one dedicated function","D":"Incapable of being connected to a network."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"An embedded system is built into a larger device to perform a specific task — a washing machine controller, engine management, a router. It has limited resources, low power draw, and firmware that rarely changes, so it can be optimised, cheap and highly reliable. Many are real-time systems where a late answer is a wrong answer. General-purpose computers trade that efficiency for the flexibility to run arbitrary software.","reforge":{"stem":"A car's airbag controller is a real-time system, meaning it must:","options":{"A":"Respond within a guaranteed time limit","B":"Run several unrelated applications at once.","C":"Store a permanent log of every journey taken.","D":"Be reprogrammed by the driver when required."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-15","stem":"Serial transmission is generally preferred to parallel over long distances because parallel transmission suffers from:","options":{"A":"An inability to carry binary data at all.","B":"A requirement for an unbroken fibre optic link.","C":"A maximum speed fixed by international standards.","D":"Skew, as bits on different wires arrive apart"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Parallel transmission sends bits simultaneously on separate wires — fast over very short distances, but over longer runs the wires' slightly different characteristics cause skew, and adjacent wires interfere through crosstalk. Serial sends one bit at a time on a single line, avoiding both, and modern serial standards run at very high clock rates. This is why USB, SATA and Ethernet are serial despite parallel appearing faster in principle.","reforge":{"stem":"Simplex transmission differs from half duplex because simplex allows data to travel:","options":{"A":"In both directions, but only one way at a time.","B":"In one direction only","C":"Only between devices on the same network.","D":"In both directions simultaneously."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-16","stem":"Increasing the bit rate of a transmission channel means it carries:","options":{"A":"More bits per second along the channel","B":"Fewer errors during each second of transmission.","C":"Signals over a greater physical distance.","D":"A more secure form of encrypted data."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Bit rate is bits transmitted per second. Baud rate is signal changes per second; where each signal change encodes more than one bit, bit rate exceeds baud rate — bit rate = baud rate × bits per signal. Bandwidth is the channel's capacity, and higher bandwidth supports a higher bit rate. Latency is delay and is independent of all three, which is why a high bit rate does not guarantee a responsive connection.","reforge":{"stem":"A channel operates at 2000 baud and encodes 4 bits per signal change. Its bit rate is:","options":{"A":"500 bits per second, dividing baud by bits.","B":"2004 bits per second, adding the two values.","C":"8000 bits per second, multiplying baud by bits","D":"2000 bits per second, since baud equals bit rate."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-17","stem":"In a star network topology, every device connects to:","options":{"A":"A single shared backbone cable running past each node.","B":"A central switch that forwards traffic between them","C":"Every other device by a dedicated individual cable.","D":"Two neighbouring devices forming a closed loop."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"Star: each device has its own link to a central switch — one cable failure affects only that device, performance is good because the switch forwards frames only to their destination, but the switch is a single point of failure. Bus: one shared backbone, cheap but collision-prone and hard to fault-find. Mesh: many redundant paths, extremely resilient but expensive in cabling, which is why it suits wireless and backbone networks.","reforge":{"stem":"A mesh topology's main advantage over a star is that it:","options":{"A":"Prevents any data collisions from occurring.","B":"Requires substantially less cabling to install.","C":"Depends on a single central switch.","D":"Provides multiple redundant paths between nodes"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N4-18","stem":"A switch differs from a hub because a switch:","options":{"A":"Assigns IP addresses to devices as they connect.","B":"Broadcasts every frame to all connected devices.","C":"Forwards each frame only to its destination port","D":"Converts between wired and wireless signalling."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"A hub repeats every frame to all ports, wasting bandwidth and causing collisions. A switch learns which MAC address sits on which port and forwards frames only there, so segments can transmit simultaneously. A router works at the network layer, connecting different networks using IP addresses and choosing routes. A gateway translates between different protocols. Exam questions rely on students conflating switch and router.","reforge":{"stem":"A router differs from a switch because a router:","options":{"A":"Connects networks using IP addresses","B":"Operates only within a single local network.","C":"Uses MAC addresses to forward every frame.","D":"Repeats incoming signals to all its ports."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N4-19","stem":"In a Wi-Fi network, the SSID identifies:","options":{"A":"The encryption standard protecting the traffic.","B":"The unique hardware address of the access point.","C":"The channel number the network transmits on.","D":"The wireless network by name"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"The SSID is the network's broadcast name. Security comes from the encryption standard: WEP is obsolete and broken, WPA2 uses AES and remains widely used, WPA3 strengthens key exchange and protects weaker passwords. Hiding the SSID or filtering MAC addresses adds only marginal difficulty since both can be observed or spoofed, so a strong passphrase with current encryption is what actually secures a wireless network.","reforge":{"stem":"Hiding a network's SSID provides only limited security because:","options":{"A":"Hidden networks cannot use any encryption.","B":"The name is still visible in device traffic","C":"The router broadcasts the passphrase instead.","D":"Devices must connect using WEP when it is hidden."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N4-20","stem":"CSMA/CA is used in wireless networks rather than CSMA/CD because wireless devices:","options":{"A":"Cannot detect collisions while transmitting","B":"Never experience interference from other signals.","C":"Transmit at a much lower overall data rate.","D":"Are always connected to a central switch."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"On wired Ethernet a device can listen while sending, so CSMA/CD detects a collision and backs off. A wireless transmitter cannot hear a weak remote signal over its own transmission, and the hidden node problem means two stations may not hear each other at all. CSMA/CA therefore avoids collisions in advance: listen, wait a random interval, optionally exchange request-to-send and clear-to-send, then transmit and await acknowledgement.","reforge":{"stem":"The hidden node problem occurs when two wireless devices:","options":{"A":"Share an identical MAC address by mistake.","B":"Use different encryption standards.","C":"Can reach the access point but not each other","D":"Are assigned the same IP address by the router."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N4-21","stem":"A protocol is best described as:","options":{"A":"A physical cable standard for connecting devices.","B":"An agreed set of rules governing communication","C":"A program that translates source code to machine code.","D":"The maximum data rate a network can sustain."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-NETWORK-PROTOCOLS","scaffold":"A protocol is an agreed set of rules covering message format, sequence, timing, addressing and error handling, so devices from different manufacturers interoperate. Common examples: HTTP and HTTPS for web pages, FTP for file transfer, SMTP for sending mail with POP3 and IMAP for retrieving it, TCP and UDP at the transport layer, IP for addressing and routing. Standards matter because without them equipment could not communicate.","reforge":{"stem":"Which protocol is used to retrieve email while leaving it stored on the server?","options":{"A":"SMTP, which is used to send outgoing mail.","B":"FTP, which transfers files between hosts.","C":"POP3, which downloads and removes messages.","D":"IMAP, which syncs mail on the server"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N4-22","stem":"Lossy compression would be inappropriate for:","options":{"A":"A photograph shared on a social media feed.","B":"A streamed music track for casual listening.","C":"A spreadsheet of financial transactions","D":"A thumbnail preview of a video clip."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Lossy compression permanently discards detail chosen to be least noticeable, giving large savings for photographs, music and video where approximate reproduction is acceptable. It is unsuitable wherever exact reconstruction matters — text, spreadsheets, program code, archives, medical images — because a changed value is simply wrong rather than slightly degraded. Repeated lossy re-encoding compounds the loss, a further reason to prefer lossless for masters.","reforge":{"stem":"Repeatedly opening and re-saving a JPEG image degrades quality because each save:","options":{"A":"Applies lossy compression to degraded data","B":"Converts the image into a vector format.","C":"Increases the image's colour depth automatically.","D":"Adds metadata that overwrites some pixels."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.3"},{"id":"CS-N5-10","stem":"An analogue-to-digital converter is required when a computer takes input from:","options":{"A":"A keyboard producing scan codes for each key.","B":"A mouse reporting movement as digital counts.","C":"A file already stored in binary on a disk.","D":"A microphone producing a varying signal"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"Physical quantities such as sound pressure, temperature and light vary continuously, so an ADC samples them at intervals and represents each sample as a binary number. Sampling rate and bit depth determine how faithfully the original is captured, and the Nyquist principle requires sampling at least twice the highest frequency present to avoid aliasing. A DAC reverses the process to drive speakers. Keyboards and mice already produce digital signals.","reforge":{"stem":"Sampling an audio signal at less than twice its highest frequency causes:","options":{"A":"An increase in the resulting file size.","B":"Aliasing, distorting the sound","C":"The bit depth to be reduced automatically.","D":"The signal to be converted back to analogue."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"},{"id":"CS-N5-11","stem":"A GPU is better suited than a CPU to rendering graphics because a GPU has:","options":{"A":"Many simple cores acting in parallel","B":"Direct access to secondary storage devices.","C":"A larger instruction set for general tasks.","D":"A much higher clock speed on a single core."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GCS-HARDWARE-ARCHITECTURE","scaffold":"A CPU has a few powerful, flexible cores optimised for sequential work with complex branching. A GPU has thousands of simpler cores designed to apply the same operation to many data items simultaneously, which suits shading pixels or transforming vertices. That same pattern makes GPUs effective for machine learning and simulation, but poor for branch-heavy sequential logic, where the CPU remains faster.","reforge":{"stem":"Which workload would gain least from being moved to a GPU?","options":{"A":"Applying one filter to every pixel of an image.","B":"Multiplying two very large matrices together.","C":"Sequential logic with heavy branching","D":"Training a neural network on a large data set."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.4"},{"id":"CS-N5-12","stem":"Unicode requires more storage per character than ASCII because Unicode:","options":{"A":"Stores each character as a compressed image.","B":"Encodes far more characters","C":"Includes a checksum with every character stored.","D":"Always uses exactly four bytes per character."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GCS-DATA-REPRESENTATION","scaffold":"ASCII uses 7 bits for 128 characters, covering English text. Unicode assigns code points to over a million characters across the world's writing systems plus symbols and emoji, so it needs more bits. UTF-8 is variable-length: one byte for characters matching ASCII, up to four for others, so English text is barely larger while other scripts remain representable. This backwards compatibility is why UTF-8 dominates on the web.","reforge":{"stem":"UTF-8 is widely used partly because a file of plain English text encoded in UTF-8 is:","options":{"A":"Always exactly half the size of the ASCII version.","B":"Stored without any character encoding at all.","C":"Automatically encrypted during transmission.","D":"Almost identical in size to the ASCII version"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"CS-4","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"eduqas-a-cs-2.2"}]}});
